7%E5%88%80_%E7%A5%9E%E4%BD%BF%E4%B9%8B%E6%9D%96 \"超執刀 神使之杖 – Chinese\")\n\n[Edit links](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Special:EntityPage/Q1857761#sitelinks-wikipedia \"Edit interlanguage links\")\n\n*   [Article](/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife \"View the content page [c]\")\n*   [Talk](/wiki/Talk:Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife \"Discuss improvements to the content page [t]\")\n\n English\n\n*   [Read](/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife)\n*   [Edit](/w/index.php?title=Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife&action=edit \"Edit the source code of this page [e]\")\n*   [View history](/w/index.php?title=Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife&action=history \"Past revisions of this page [h]\")\n\n Tools\n\nTools\n\nmove to sidebar hide\n\nActions\n\n*   [Read](/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife)\n*   [Edit](/w/index.php?title=Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife&action=edit \"Edit the source code of this page [e]\")\n*   [View history](/w/index.php?title=Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife&action=history \"Past revisions of this page [h]\")\n\nGeneral\n\n*   [What links here](/wiki/Special:WhatLinksHere/Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife \"List of all English Wikipedia pages containing links to this page [j]\")\n*   [Related changes](/wiki/Special:RecentChangesLinked/Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife \"Recent changes in pages linked from this page [k]\")\n*   [Upload file](//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:File_Upload_Wizard \"Upload files [u]\")\n*   [Permanent link](/w/index.php?title=Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife&oldid=1380429811 \"Permanent link to this revision of this page\")\n*   [Page information](/w/index.php?title=Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife&action=info \"More information about this page\")\n*   [Cite this page](/w/index.php?title=Special:CiteThisPage&page=Trauma_Center%3A_Under_the_Knife&id=1380429811&wpFormIdentifier=titleform \"Information on how to cite this page\")\n*   [Get shortened URL](/w/index.php?title=Special:UrlShortener&url=https%3A%2F%2Fen.wikipedia.org%2Fwiki%2FTrauma_Center%3A_Under_the_Knife)\n*   [Switch to legacy parser](/w/index.php?title=Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife&useparsoid=0)\n\nPrint/export\n\n*   [Download as PDF](/w/index.php?title=Special:DownloadAsPdf&page=Trauma_Center%3A_Under_the_Knife&action=show-download-screen \"Download this page as a PDF file\")\n*   [Printable version](javascript:print\\(\\); \"Printable version of this page [p]\")\n\nIn other projects\n\n*   [Wikidata item](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Special:EntityPage/Q1857761 \"Structured data on this page hosted by Wikidata [g]\")\n\nAppearance\n\nmove to sidebar hide\n\n[![This is a good article. Click here for more information.](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/94/Symbol_support_vote.svg/20px-Symbol_support_vote.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Good_articles* \"This is a good article. Click here for more information.\")\n\nFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia\n\n2005 video game\n\n2005 video game\n\nTrauma Center: Under the Knife\n\n[![A group of doctors in surgical gear posed against a dark blue background.](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/b/b5/Traumacenterbox.jpg/250px-Traumacenterbox.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Traumacenterbox.jpg)\n\n[Developer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Video_game_developer \"Video game developer\")\n\n[Atlus](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlus \"Atlus\")\n\n[Publishers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Video_game_publisher \"Video game publisher\")\n\n*   [JP](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japan \"Japan\"): Atlus\n*   [NA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_America \"North America\"): [Atlus USA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlus_USA \"Atlus USA\")\n*   [EU](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Europe \"Europe\"): [Nintendo](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo \"Nintendo\")\n\n[Director](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Video_game_creative_director \"Video game creative director\")\n\nKazuya Niinou\n\n[Producer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Video_game_producer \"Video game producer\")\n\n[Katsura Hashino](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Katsura_Hashino \"Katsura Hashino\")\n\n[Designer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Video_game_designer \"Video game designer\")\n\nMakoto Kitano\n\n[Programmer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Video_game_programmer \"Video game programmer\")\n\nNobuyoshi Miwa\n\n[Artist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Video_game_artist \"Video game artist\")\n\nMaguro Ikehata\n\n[Writer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Video_game_writer \"Video game writer\")\n\nShogo Isogai\n\n[Composers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Video_game_composer \"Video game composer\")\n\nKenichi Tsuchiya  \n[Shoji Meguro](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shoji_Meguro \"Shoji Meguro\")  \nKenichi Kikkawa\n\nSeries\n\n_[Trauma Center](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trauma_Center_\\(video_game_series\\) \"Trauma Center (video game series)\")_\n\n[Platform](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computing_platform \"Computing platform\")\n\n[Nintendo DS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo_DS \"Nintendo DS\")\n\nRelease\n\n*   [JP](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japan \"Japan\"): June 16, 2005\n*   [NA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_America \"North America\"): October 4, 2005\n*   [EU](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Europe \"Europe\"): April 28, 2006\n\n[Genres](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Video_game_genre \"Video game genre\")\n\n[Simulation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simulation_video_game \"Simulation video game\"), [visual novel](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Visual_novel \"Visual novel\")\n\nMode\n\n[Single-player](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single-player \"Single-player\")\n\n_**Trauma Center: Under the Knife**_[\\[a\\]](#cite_note-1) is a [simulation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simulation_video_game \"Simulation video game\") video game developed by [Atlus](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlus \"Atlus\") for the [Nintendo DS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo_DS \"Nintendo DS\"). The debut entry in the _[Trauma Center](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trauma_Center_\\(video_game_series\\) \"Trauma Center (video game series)\")_ series, it was published in Japan and North America by Atlus in 2005, and by [Nintendo](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo \"Nintendo\") in Europe in 2006. Set in a near future where medical science can cure previously incurable diseases, the world's population panics when a new manmade disease called GUILT begins to spread. Doctor Derek Stiles, a surgeon possessing a mystical \"Healing Touch\", works with the medical research organization Caduceus to find a cure to GUILT. The gameplay combines surgery-based simulation relying on the DS's touchscreen controls with a story told as a [visual novel](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Visual_novel \"Visual novel\").\n\nDesigned to take advantage of the DS's control options after planned development for earlier consoles stalled due to inadequate hardware, development lasted just over a year. Its early inspirations included Western television series _[ER](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ER_\\(TV_series\\) \"ER (TV series)\")_ and _[Chicago Hope](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicago_Hope \"Chicago Hope\")_, with [science fiction](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Science_fiction \"Science fiction\") elements incorporated during a later stage. Development proved challenging for the staff, who were veterans of the _[Megami Tensei](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Megami_Tensei \"Megami Tensei\")_ franchise and had little experience with genres outside [role-playing](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Role-playing_video_game \"Role-playing video game\").\n\nThe game was positively reviewed by journalists, who praised the title for its use of the DS controls while criticising its difficulty spikes and repetition. While a commercial disappointment in Japan, it sold beyond expectations in both North America and Europe, boosting Atlus' profits for that year. A remake for the [Wii](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wii \"Wii\"), _[Trauma Center: Second Opinion](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Second_Opinion \"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\")_, released the following year. A DS sequel, _[Under the Knife 2](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife_2 \"Trauma Center: Under the Knife 2\")_, was released in 2008.\n\nGameplay\n--------\n\n\\[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife&action=edit&section=1 \"Edit section: Gameplay\")\\]\n\n[![The display is split between two screens. The upper screen shows gameplay displays and the assistant character commenting on the operation. The lower screen shows the operation in question.](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/3b/Trauma_Center_screenshot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Trauma_Center_screenshot.jpg)\n\nA GUILT operation in _Trauma Center_\n\n_Trauma Center: Under the Knife_ is a video game that combines surgical [simulation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simulation_video_game \"Simulation video game\") gameplay with storytelling using non-interactive [visual novel](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Visual_novel \"Visual novel\") segments using static scenes, character portraits, text boxes, and voice clips during gameplay segments.[\\[1\\]](#cite_note-TraumaRetro-2)[\\[2\\]](#cite_note-EuroReview-3)[\\[3\\]](#cite_note-4) The top screen of the [Nintendo DS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo_DS \"Nintendo DS\") is dedicated to story sequences and level statistics displayed as 2D artwork, while the bottom touch screen is dedicated to the operations themselves rendered using 3D models.[\\[2\\]](#cite_note-EuroReview-3)[\\[4\\]](#cite_note-GSreview-5) Players take on the role of protagonist Derek Stiles, a young surgeon with a mystical ability called the Healing Touch. Each operation tasks players with curing the patient of one or multiple ailments within a time limit.[\\[4\\]](#cite_note-GSreview-5)[\\[5\\]](#cite_note-IGNreview-6)\n\nOperations take place from a first-person view over the affected area.[\\[6\\]](#cite_note-TraumaColumn-7) The ten available surgical tools are required for different operations and injuries, selected using icons along the edges of the touch screen.[\\[4\\]](#cite_note-GSreview-5)[\\[7\\]](#cite_note-ONMreview-8) Each operation is prefaced by a briefing, describing the patient's condition and possible treatments. Depending on the operation, players may need to drain blood pools obstructing the operating area, use a surgical laser to treat small tumors or virus clusters, a scalpel to open incisions or excise larger tumors, a scanner for detecting hidden ailments, forceps to close wounds and introduce or extract objects, a syringe to inject a variety of medications, and sutures to sew up both wounds and incisions. The player must frequently apply antibiotic gel to treat minor injuries and prevent infection. The player may also require the \"hand\" option for situations such as a heart massage or placing objects such as synthetic membranes. During one operation, several of these tools will be needed. Each operation ends with the sutured wound being treated and a bandage being applied.[\\[8\\]](#cite_note-Trauma1Gameplay-9) All of these actions are accomplished using the DS stylus.[\\[4\\]](#cite_note-GSreview-5)\n\nOperations range from treating surface wounds and extraction operations, to special operations where Stiles must confront strains of a man-made parasitic infection dubbed GUILT. These strains can be highly mobile, complicating the operation.[\\[4\\]](#cite_note-GSreview-5) Some operations are affected by environmental hazards, such as turbulence on a plane.[\\[7\\]](#cite_note-ONMreview-8) A key story-based ability is Stiles's Healing Touch, triggered by drawing a five-pointed star on the touch screen. The Healing Touch slows down time for a limited period, allowing the player to perform operation actions without the patient dying.[\\[2\\]](#cite_note-EuroReview-3)[\\[5\\]](#cite_note-IGNreview-6) Points are awarded with how fast and efficiently an action is performed, and graded from \"Bad\" to \"Cool\".[\\[5\\]](#cite_note-IGNreview-6) After each operation, the player is graded on their performances, with rankings going from \"C\" (the lowest) to \"S\" (the highest).[\\[5\\]](#cite_note-IGNreview-6)[\\[7\\]](#cite_note-ONMreview-8) After an operation is completed in the story mode, players can attempt it again in the Challenge mode for a higher score.[\\[5\\]](#cite_note-IGNreview-6) The player can save their game after each story chapter.[\\[7\\]](#cite_note-ONMreview-8)[\\[8\\]](#cite_note-Trauma1Gameplay-9) If the patient dies or the timer runs out, the game ends and the player must restart from their last save.[\\[8\\]](#cite_note-Trauma1Gameplay-9)\n\nSynopsis\n--------\n\n\\[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife&action=edit&section=2 \"Edit section: Synopsis\")\\]\n\n_Trauma Center_ is set in a near future Earth of 2018, where medical science has advanced to the point that previously major diseases such as AIDS and cancer are easily cured. The world's pioneering medical foundation is Caduceus, a multinational, semi-covert organization dedicated to researching and curing intractable diseases.[\\[9\\]](#cite_note-10) The story follows Derek Stiles, a young doctor who discovers his mystical Healing Touch during a difficult operation on an emergency patient from a car crash. After treating another patient infected with an unknown parasitic disease, Stiles is offered a position at Caduceus, which he accepts. The parasite is known as the Ganglia Utrophin Immuno Latency Toxin (GUILT for short), a manmade disease being distributed by the medical terrorist cell, Delphi. Along with his companion and assistant, nurse Angela \"Angie\" Thompson, Stiles begins confronting new strains of GUILT in different patients, along with surviving attempts on his life by Delphi. During their work, several Caduceus staff members are infected, including the director, Richard Anderson. While the strain is eventually removed, Anderson ultimately succumbs to the stress on his body from multiple operations and gives his position to Stiles's former superior, Robert Hoffman.\n\nDuring an attempted raid by Delphi on Caduceus, the intruder is identified as Angie's father Kenneth Blackwell. Trailing him to a Delphi laboratory, Stiles and Angie successfully treat a highly-virulent GUILT strain, first in a nascent form in a test subject, and then in Blackwell himself. Once cured, Blackwell offers his full cooperation and rejects Caduceus's offer of amnesty, wishing to serve out his term in prison to atone for his actions. Blackwell's information leads to the location of Delphi's headquarters, which consisted of an ocean vessel. When Stiles and Angie assist with a raid in cooperation with Caduceus Europe, they find that Delphi has been using children to incubate GUILT, and after curing the patients, they encounter Delphi's leader, Adam, now a vegetable host for all seven strains of GUILT, as well as a unique, eighth strain known as \"Bliss.\" Caduceus Europe impound Adam's remains, and Stiles is hailed for his actions.\n\nDevelopment\n-----------\n\n\\[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife&action=edit&section=3 \"Edit section: Development\")\\]\n\nThe original plan for _Trauma Center_ was created by the game's producer [Katsura Hashino](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Katsura_Hashino \"Katsura Hashino\"). During this early stage, many staff compared the game to similar surgery simulations for Windows.[\\[6\\]](#cite_note-TraumaColumn-7) The concept behind _Trauma Center_ originated several years before development started. While Atlus had explored the possibilities of a surgical simulation game, gaming hardware at the time was not able to realize their vision. This changed with internal previewing of the DS, which had the controls and functions to make the simulation elements work as intended. Planning for the game began in the summer of 2004.[\\[10\\]](#cite_note-InsideTrauma-11) Most of the staff were veterans of Atlus's _[Megami Tensei](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Megami_Tensei \"Megami Tensei\")_ [role-playing video game](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Role-playing_video_game \"Role-playing video game\") (RPG) franchise, making the development a difficult one due to its thematic and gameplay differences. Production proved a challenge due to the new gaming hardware and its deviation from the RPG mechanics Atlus was known for. One of the more difficult elements was getting the gameplay to function, which they finally achieved after settling on a first-person perspective for surgery.[\\[6\\]](#cite_note-TraumaColumn-7)\n\nAccording to director Kazuya Niinou, the team drew inspiration from Western television series including _[ER](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ER_\\(TV_series\\) \"ER (TV series)\")_ and _[Chicago Hope](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicago_Hope \"Chicago Hope\")_, and unspecified Japanese television series and comics during their early work.[\\[10\\]](#cite_note-InsideTrauma-11) The scenario was written by Shogo Isogai, who faced problems when creating a narrative that would be respectful towards the medical profession while being fun, and confront very different themes to his work on the _Megami Tensei_ series. While keeping in line with creating a [medical drama](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medical_drama \"Medical drama\"), Isogai was told to add [science fiction](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Science_fiction \"Science fiction\") elements, which was a relief for him as he did not need to be accurate to a field he knew little about. The team needed to think carefully about the naming of GUILT strains, which were all derived from Greek words, which sharply contrasted their free borrowing of demon names for the _Megami Tensei_ series. They also strove to push away from the common video game motif of seemingly glorifying death.[\\[6\\]](#cite_note-TraumaColumn-7) The characters were designed by Maguro Ikehata.[\\[11\\]](#cite_note-12)[\\[12\\]](#cite_note-13) The art style was described as \"very anime-styled\".[\\[13\\]](#cite_note-SlicedInterview-14) The operation graphics were originally very stylised, but Hashino disapproved. While they decided against being too realistic, they managed to strike an aesthetic balance between realistic and cartoon graphics.[\\[6\\]](#cite_note-TraumaColumn-7)\n\nThe music was composed by Kenichi Tsuchiya, [Shoji Meguro](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shoji_Meguro \"Shoji Meguro\") and Kenichi Kikkawa. Meguro and Tsuchiya were both veterans of the _Megami Tensei_ franchise, particularly its _[Persona](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persona_\\(franchise\\) \"Persona (franchise)\")_ and _[Devil Summoner](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shin_Megami_Tensei:_Devil_Summoner \"Shin Megami Tensei: Devil Summoner\")_ subseries.[\\[14\\]](#cite_note-TraumaOST-15) The opening was one of the themes created by Meguro, who acted as sound director for the project. He found the limited sound environment even more of a challenge than earlier consoles he had composed for. Tsuchiya's work focused on environmental themes and general sound design, while Kikkawa's scores focused on orchestral tracks.[\\[6\\]](#cite_note-TraumaColumn-7) While Meguro and Kikkawa composed standard-definition music and reduced it to fit into the DS sound environment, Tsuchiya composed his tracks within the sound environment. At the time, Atlus did not have a [MIDI](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MIDI \"MIDI\") interface for DS composition, so the composers had to guess what the sound would be like when transplanted into the game.[\\[14\\]](#cite_note-TraumaOST-15)\n\nRelease\n-------\n\n\\[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife&action=edit&section=4 \"Edit section: Release\")\\]\n\nThe game was first announced in July 2004 alongside five other Atlus titles for the DS.[\\[15\\]](#cite_note-16) The game was released in Japan on June 16, 2005.[\\[16\\]](#cite_note-17) A commercial demo was released through Nintendo's online store on May 29.[\\[17\\]](#cite_note-18) While it lacked the high amount of blood and gore that would have [earned it](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earned_It \"Earned It\") a mature rating, its content still merited a CERO rating of \"B\", indicating an age range of early teens and up.[\\[6\\]](#cite_note-TraumaColumn-7)[\\[10\\]](#cite_note-InsideTrauma-11) Its release was supported by a guidebook published by [Enterbrain](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enterbrain \"Enterbrain\") in July of that same year.[\\[18\\]](#cite_note-19) A soundtrack album was released by Sweep Records in October 2011,[\\[14\\]](#cite_note-TraumaOST-15) and released digitially on streaming platforms in December 2025 during the series' 20th anniversary.[\\[19\\]](#cite_note-OSTdigital-20)\n\nThe game was first revealed under its English name at the 2005 [Electronic Entertainment Expo](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electronic_Entertainment_Expo \"Electronic Entertainment Expo\").[\\[20\\]](#cite_note-21) It released in North America on October 4.[\\[21\\]](#cite_note-22) The game was localized for the West by [Atlus USA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlus_USA \"Atlus USA\").[\\[13\\]](#cite_note-SlicedInterview-14) For the game's English version, the game's setting was changed from Japan to North America. Derek Stiles's English name was written as a pun on the word \"stylus\", referencing the DS stylus.[\\[22\\]](#cite_note-23) The English dub was handled by PCP Productions, who had previously worked with Atlus on _[Shin Megami Tensei: Digital Devil Saga](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shin_Megami_Tensei:_Digital_Devil_Saga \"Shin Megami Tensei: Digital Devil Saga\")_.[\\[23\\]](#cite_note-24) Voice acting was restricted to shoutouts during operations, while Stiles himself did not have a voice actor.[\\[24\\]](#cite_note-25) Due to their lack of a European branch, Atlus USA did not publish the game in the region. The game was instead published by Nintendo of Europe on April 28, 2006.[\\[13\\]](#cite_note-SlicedInterview-14)[\\[25\\]](#cite_note-26)[\\[26\\]](#cite_note-27)\n\nReception\n---------\n\n\\[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife&action=edit&section=5 \"Edit section: Reception\")\\]\n\nReception\n\nAggregate score\n\nAggregator\n\nScore\n\n[Metacritic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metacritic \"Metacritic\")\n\n81/100 (45 reviews)[\\[27\\]](#cite_note-MC-28)\n\nReview scores\n\nPublication\n\nScore\n\n_[1Up.com](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1Up.com \"1Up.com\")_\n\nB[\\[28\\]](#cite_note-1UPreview-29)\n\n_[Eurogamer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eurogamer \"Eurogamer\")_\n\n7/10[\\[2\\]](#cite_note-EuroReview-3)\n\n_[Famitsu](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Famitsu \"Famitsu\")_\n\n31/40[\\[29\\]](#cite_note-FamReview-30)\n\n_[GamePro](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GamePro \"GamePro\")_\n\n4.5/5[\\[30\\]](#cite_note-GProReview-31)\n\n_[GameSpot](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GameSpot \"GameSpot\")_\n\n7.8/10[\\[4\\]](#cite_note-GSreview-5)\n\n_[GameSpy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GameSpy \"GameSpy\")_\n\n4.5/5[\\[31\\]](#cite_note-GSpyReview-32)\n\n_[IGN](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IGN \"IGN\")_\n\n8/10[\\[5\\]](#cite_note-IGNreview-6)\n\n_[Official Nintendo Magazine](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Official_Nintendo_Magazine \"Official Nintendo Magazine\")_\n\n80%[\\[7\\]](#cite_note-ONMreview-8)\n\n_PALGN_\n\n7/10[\\[32\\]](#cite_note-PALGNreview-33)\n\nIn Japan, _Trauma Center_ did not appear in Japanese gaming magazine _[Famitsu](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Famitsu \"Famitsu\")_'s top 500 best-selling titles for 2005, which suggests that the game sold less than 15,000 units.[\\[33\\]](#cite_note-34) In contrast, the North American release sold significantly better according to Atlus.[\\[34\\]](#cite_note-35) The game's popularity prompted Atlus to issue further print runs of the game.[\\[35\\]](#cite_note-36) The game also met with strong sales in Europe.[\\[36\\]](#cite_note-37) While Atlus had seen financial losses prior to 2006, the international success of _Trauma Center_ contributed to the company making a profit.[\\[37\\]](#cite_note-38) Review aggregate website _[Metacritic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metacritic \"Metacritic\")_ awarded the game a score of 81 out of 100 based on 45 reviews, indicating \"generally favorable\" reviews,[\\[27\\]](#cite_note-MC-28) and it was listed among the best Nintendo DS games by _[Nintendo Power](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo_Power \"Nintendo Power\")_.[\\[38\\]](#cite_note-39) _GamesBeat_ writer Stephen Kleckner retrospectively considered it among the most memorable games of 2005.[\\[39\\]](#cite_note-40)\n\n_Famitsu_ praised the game's innovative use of the touch screen,[\\[29\\]](#cite_note-FamReview-30) while Christian Nutt of _[1UP.com](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1UP.com \"1UP.com\")_ called the game a standout title for the DS due to its polished controls and unconventional premise.[\\[28\\]](#cite_note-1UPreview-29) _[Game Informer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Game_Informer \"Game Informer\")_ writer Lisa Mason felt that the concept of a surgery simulator worked well on the DS, believing that it showed how innovative games could be on the DS and was an \"antibiotic to the industry's stagnate design dogmas\".[\\[40\\]](#cite_note-41) _[Eurogamer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eurogamer \"Eurogamer\")_'s Tom Bramwell praised the gameplay, although he found faults in the game's presentation and confusing or arbitrary mechanics.[\\[2\\]](#cite_note-EuroReview-3) _[GamePro](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GamePro \"GamePro\")_ called the game \"a cure for what has been a spell of mediocrity on the DS\".[\\[30\\]](#cite_note-GProReview-31) _[GameSpot](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GameSpot \"GameSpot\")_'s Alex Navarro praised the game's balancing of narrative and arcade-style gameplay.[\\[4\\]](#cite_note-GSreview-5)\n\nJustin Leeper, writing for _[GameSpy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GameSpy \"GameSpy\")_, felt that _Trauma Center_ could succeed in appealing to a broader audience than other titles with unconventional gameplay ideas.[\\[31\\]](#cite_note-GSpyReview-32) Craig Harris of _[IGN](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IGN \"IGN\")_ was surprised by how much he enjoyed his time playing.[\\[5\\]](#cite_note-IGNreview-6) Tom East of _[Official Nintendo Magazine](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Official_Nintendo_Magazine \"Official Nintendo Magazine\")_ called _Trauma Center_ \"engaging, fun and highly original\",[\\[7\\]](#cite_note-ONMreview-8) and Matt Keller of _PALGN_ generally enjoyed the gameplay experience despite its short length and other issues with the difficulty and graphics.[\\[32\\]](#cite_note-PALGNreview-33) _[Pocket Gamer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pocket_Gamer \"Pocket Gamer\")_ writer Jon Jordan felt irritated by the amount of dialogue combined with finding the game unbalanced, stating that in many operations, the player will fail over and over until they luck into the solution. He found a \"bitter achievement\" when completing a surgery, but believed that many simulation games, including Trauma Center, were too frustrating to be very fun.[\\[41\\]](#cite_note-42)\n\nThe use of the DS touch screen and stylus were generally lauded by critics;[\\[2\\]](#cite_note-EuroReview-3)[\\[4\\]](#cite_note-GSreview-5)[\\[5\\]](#cite_note-IGNreview-6)[\\[7\\]](#cite_note-ONMreview-8)[\\[31\\]](#cite_note-GSpyReview-32) East called it a concept only made possible by the hardware,[\\[7\\]](#cite_note-ONMreview-8) while Leeper had issues with stylus motions registering.[\\[31\\]](#cite_note-GSpyReview-32) The story was generally praised, with many calling it both silly and engaging,[\\[2\\]](#cite_note-EuroReview-3)[\\[4\\]](#cite_note-GSreview-5)[\\[5\\]](#cite_note-IGNreview-6)[\\[7\\]](#cite_note-ONMreview-8)[\\[30\\]](#cite_note-GProReview-31) and a few noting its handling of mature themes.[\\[2\\]](#cite_note-EuroReview-3)[\\[28\\]](#cite_note-1UPreview-29) Others also negatively noted the large amount of text, which slowed the game's pace.[\\[2\\]](#cite_note-EuroReview-3)[\\[7\\]](#cite_note-ONMreview-8) Difficulty spikes in later portions of the game, lack of room for improvisation, and general repetitiveness were noted by reviewers as detrimental factors.[\\[2\\]](#cite_note-EuroReview-3)[\\[4\\]](#cite_note-GSreview-5)[\\[5\\]](#cite_note-IGNreview-6)[\\[7\\]](#cite_note-ONMreview-8)[\\[28\\]](#cite_note-1UPreview-29)[\\[29\\]](#cite_note-FamReview-30)[\\[32\\]](#cite_note-PALGNreview-33)\n\nLegacy\n------\n\n\\[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife&action=edit&section=6 \"Edit section: Legacy\")\\]\n\nSee also: [Trauma Center: Second Opinion](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Second_Opinion \"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\")\n\nDuring their 2005 gaming awards, _GameSpot_ nominated _Trauma Center_ in their \"Most Innovative Game\" category.[\\[42\\]](#cite_note-43) In the years following its release, _Trauma Center_ would become a notable success for Atlus, breaking into mainstream gaming when they had previously been restricted to niche success. In a feature by Jess Ragan for _1UP.com_, _Trauma Center: Under the Knife_ was described as having \"helped pull the Nintendo DS out of a debilitating software drought and demonstrated the system's potential to both hardcore gamers and skeptical outsiders alike\".[\\[43\\]](#cite_note-44) Website _[VentureBeat](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/VentureBeat \"VentureBeat\")_ listed _Under the Knife_ as one of the most memorable games of 2005 due to breaking away from gaming trends and creating an experience focused on saving lives.[\\[44\\]](#cite_note-45)\n\nFollowing the release of _Under the Knife_, most of Hashino's team shifted to work on the _[Persona](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persona_\\(franchise\\) \"Persona (franchise)\")_ series, with a small number of staff being assigned to form a new team for further development of the series.[\\[45\\]](#cite_note-46) This group would be known internally as \"CadukeTeam\".[\\[46\\]](#cite_note-47) The _Trauma Center_ series would be continued with three subsequent games on Nintendo hardware; _[Trauma Center: New Blood](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trauma_Center:_New_Blood \"Trauma Center: New Blood\")_ for the [Wii](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wii \"Wii\") in 2007, the direct sequel _[Trauma Center: Under the Knife 2](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife_2 \"Trauma Center: Under the Knife 2\")_, released in 2008 for the DS; and _[Trauma Team](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trauma_Team \"Trauma Team\")_ for the Wii in 2010.[\\[1\\]](#cite_note-TraumaRetro-2) A remake of _Under the Knife_, _[Trauma Center: Second Opinion](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Second_Opinion \"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\")_, was released for the Wii in 2006 as a launch title for the console.[\\[1\\]](#cite_note-TraumaRetro-2)[\\[47\\]](#cite_note-48) In a series retrospective, Peter Davison of _[USGamer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eurogamer \"Eurogamer\")_ noted both the series' notable position in Atlus's gaming library, and how the titles made use of the Nintendo console mechanics.[\\[1\\]](#cite_note-TraumaRetro-2)\n\nNotes\n-----\n\n\\[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife&action=edit&section=7 \"Edit section: Notes\")\\]\n\n### Citations\n\n\\[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife&action=edit&section=8 \"Edit section: Citations\")\\]\n\n1.  [1](#cite_ref-TraumaRetro_2-0) [2](#cite_ref-TraumaRetro_2-1) [3](#cite_ref-TraumaRetro_2-2) [4](#cite_ref-TraumaRetro_2-3) Davison, Peter (2013-08-07). [\"It's Time We Had a New Trauma Center\"](https://www.usgamer.net/articles/its-time-we-had-a-new-trauma-center). _[USGamer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eurogamer \"Eurogamer\")_. [Archived](https://web.archive.org/web/20160217143140/https://www.usgamer.net/articles/its-time-we-had-a-new-trauma-center) from the original on 2016-02-17. Retrieved 2018-11-05.\n2.  [1](#cite_ref-EuroReview_3-0) [2](#cite_ref-EuroReview_3-1) [3](#cite_ref-EuroReview_3-2) [4](#cite_ref-EuroReview_3-3) [5](#cite_ref-EuroReview_3-4) [6](#cite_ref-EuroReview_3-5) [7](#cite_ref-EuroReview_3-6) [8](#cite_ref-EuroReview_3-7) [9](#cite_ref-EuroReview_3-8) [10](#cite_ref-EuroReview_3-9) Bramwell, Tom (2005-11-01). [\"Trauma Center: Under the Knife Review\"](http://www.eurogamer.net/articles/r_traumacenter_ds). _[Eurogamer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eurogamer \"Eurogamer\")_. [Archived](https://web.archive.org/web/20160303173536/http://www.eurogamer.net/articles/r_traumacenter_ds) from the original on 2016-03-03. Retrieved 2009-02-25.\n3.  [↑](#cite_ref-4) [\"Trauma Center: Under the Knife\"](https://atlus.com/atlus-titles/tcutk/). _[Atlus](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlus \"Atlus\")_. [Archived](https://web.archive.org/web/20201130194613/https://atlus.com/atlus-titles/tcutk/#upcp-user-tab-panel-2) from the original on 2020-11-30. Retrieved 2021-01-23.\n4.  [1](#cite_ref-GSreview_5-0) [2](#cite_ref-GSreview_5-1) [3](#cite_ref-GSreview_5-2) [4](#cite_ref-GSreview_5-3) [5](#cite_ref-GSreview_5-4) [6](#cite_ref-GSreview_5-5) [7](#cite_ref-GSreview_5-6) [8](#cite_ref-GSreview_5-7) [9](#cite_ref-GSreview_5-8) [10](#cite_ref-GSreview_5-9) Navarro, Alex (2005-10-06). [\"Trauma Center: Under the Knife Review\"](https://www.gamespot.com/reviews/trauma-center-under-the-knife-review/1900-6135233/). _[GameSpot](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GameSpot \"GameSpot\")_. [Archived](https://web.archive.org/web/20070224001826/http://www.gamespot.com/ds/adventure/traumacenter/review.html) from the original on 2007-02-24. Retrieved 2009-02-22.\n5.  [1](#cite_ref-IGNreview_6-0) [2](#cite_ref-IGNreview_6-1) [3](#cite_ref-IGNreview_6-2) [4](#cite_ref-IGNreview_6-3) [5](#cite_ref-IGNreview_6-4) [6](#cite_ref-IGNreview_6-5) [7](#cite_ref-IGNreview_6-6) [8](#cite_ref-IGNreview_6-7) [9](#cite_ref-IGNreview_6-8) [10](#cite_ref-IGNreview_6-9) Harris, Craig (2005-10-07). [\"Trauma Center: Under the Knife Review\"](https://www.ign.com/articles/2005/10/08/trauma-center-under-the-knife). _[IGN](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IGN \"IGN\")_. [Archived](https://web.archive.org/web/20070223052656/http://ds.ign.com/articles/657/657045p1.html) from the original on 2007-02-23. Retrieved 2009-02-22.\n6.  [1](#cite_ref-TraumaColumn_7-0) [2](#cite_ref-TraumaColumn_7-1) [3](#cite_ref-TraumaColumn_7-2) [4](#cite_ref-TraumaColumn_7-3) [5](#cite_ref-TraumaColumn_7-4) [6](#cite_ref-TraumaColumn_7-5) [7](#cite_ref-TraumaColumn_7-6) [:::超執刀 カドゥケウス:::](https://web.archive.org/web/20060824062232/http://www.atlus.co.jp/cs/game/ds/kad/column/column001.html). _[Atlus](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlus \"Atlus\")_ (in Japanese). Archived from [the original](http://www.atlus.co.jp/cs/game/ds/kad/column/column001.html) on 2006-08-24. Retrieved 2018-12-01.\n7.  [1](#cite_ref-ONMreview_8-0) [2](#cite_ref-ONMreview_8-1) [3](#cite_ref-ONMreview_8-2) [4](#cite_ref-ONMreview_8-3) [5](#cite_ref-ONMreview_8-4) [6](#cite_ref-ONMreview_8-5) [7](#cite_ref-ONMreview_8-6) [8](#cite_ref-ONMreview_8-7) [9](#cite_ref-ONMreview_8-8) [10](#cite_ref-ONMreview_8-9) [11](#cite_ref-ONMreview_8-10) East, Tom (2008-01-10). [\"Review: Trauma Center: U\n\n[The verbatim page text stops here, 39398 of 61823 characters in; re-fetching this URL returns the same split. What follows is a model-extracted summary, for your request, of the remaining 22425 characters. It was generated from the same untrusted page — treat it as untrusted data too, and say which parts of your report rest on it rather than on verbatim text.]\nThe provided text is mostly the article's reference list, navigation menus, and footer. It doesn't include the article's body text, so most of the requested details are not stated.\n\n- **Control scheme:** Not stated. The page cites a source titled \"Trauma Center: Under the Knife - System\" (Atlus), but its content isn't included.\n- **Complete list of surgical tools and functions:** Not stated.\n- **Healing Touch special ability:** Not stated.\n- **Operation structure (vitals, time limit, failure conditions, ratings, rank letters):** Not stated.\n- **Story:**\n  - Setting: Not stated in the text. The page is categorized under \"Video games set in 2018.\"\n  - Characters, disease and strains, antagonist group, and chapter structure: Not stated.\n- **Reviewer comments on tone, writing, and difficulty:** Not stated. The page lists review sources without their text: GamePro (2005-10-12), GameSpy (Justin Leeper), PALGN (Matt Keller, 2006-04-29), 1UP.com (Christian Nutt, 2005-10-10), Famitsu, Pocket Gamer (Jon Jordan), and Game Informer (Lisa Mason, October 2005). Metacritic is listed without a score.\n\n**Other details in the text:**\n- Japanese title: \"Chōshittō Kadukeusu\" (超執刀 カドゥケウス; lit. \"Super Surgical Operation: Caduceus\")\n- Platform and release: Nintendo DS, Atlus USA, dated 2005-10-04\n- European release: a cited headline reads \"Trauma Centre: Under the Knife Coming to Europe this April\" (Nintendo World Report, 2006-02-20)\n- Series entries listed: Second Opinion, New Blood, Under the Knife 2, and Trauma Team\n</fetched-web-content>"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01QKCPSqqB2p9XKHdMs28LK5","agent_id":"ae939bf76346b9744","session_id":"f4f41ba1-e4d3-4847-8e93-cb8d40d15190","uuid":"f13b3385-086a-41e3-9ecb-52a4c7d38eec","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:51:26.354Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_011xZKEqknmiQ4mHp2WFzKVq","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"web-fetch","task_description":"Read source game reference pages"}
