{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Yax6QE9YmYVvAEtUnWUjpM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Game Plan: a surgical action game inspired by *Trauma Center: Second Opinion*\n2\t\n3\tYou are building this game from this document, without its author. Your brief comes first; this plan turns the brief's open choices into decisions. Where the plan is silent, choose whatever serves the pillars in §1. Don't stop to ask.\n4\t\n5\t- **Title:** yours. Pick it the way your brief says and keep it in one file, `src/config/identity.js`. No other source file, line of dialogue or asset may contain it. The logo must fit any title length.\n6\t- **File and folder names:** your brief names the dev folder, the final HTML file and the exit interview file. This plan never does. Every path below is relative to the dev folder.\n7\t- **Verified recipes:** everything in §16 was run on this computer on 2026-11-04 with the exact versions named. If a recipe disagrees with your instincts, trust the recipe.\n8\t- **Contents:** §1 the game · §2 world and cast · §3 core rules · §4 tools · §5 strains · §6 operations · §7 story · §8 controls and screens · §9 art · §10 animation · §11 audio · §12 attract mode · §13 dev mode · §14 multiplayer · §15 architecture · §16 verified recipes and traps · §17 testing · §18 build order · §19 About screen, exit interview, catalogue · §20 brief coverage checklist\n9\t\n10\t---\n11\t\n12\t## 1. The game\n13\t\n14\t**Genre:** surgical action (arcade operation game) with a visual-novel story between operations.\n15\t\n16\t**Perspective:** first-person surgeon's-eye view of a 3D operative field, from a fixed, tilted camera (§9.2). Story scenes are staged third-person two-shots of 3D character busts.\n17\t\n18\t**Pitch:** a rain-soaked medical soap opera where a nervous young surgeon fights an engineered parasite that keeps time with the human heart. Each operation is 3 to 7 minutes of tool-switching panic: cut, pull, drain, burn, stitch, while the patient's heart races, the music speeds up with it, and a head nurse tells you exactly what she thinks of your hands. Then a rank stamp slams down on the chart, and the next envelope of sheet music arrives.\n19\t\n20\t**The hook, which is ours and not the source's: the heartbeat is the clock.** One beat clock drives the ECG, the tissue's squeeze, the music's tempo, the parasites' attacks and the controller's rumble. As vitals fall, the heart races, so everything speeds up. Every parasite is a piece of music, drawn in musical notation, and it plays the patient's heart like an instrument.\n21\t\n22\t### Pillars\n23\t\n24\t1. **Every second is a heartbeat.** If something moves, sounds or attacks, it does so on the beat.\n25\t2. **Hands you can feel.** Analog trigger pressure, physical pulls with real tension, the pop, the arc to the tray, the clink. Objects you remove stay piled on the tray as trophies.\n26\t3. **Soap-opera sincerity.** Earnest, melodramatic, medically absurd, and never winking.\n27\t4. **One more try.** Hard, but never a spike: checkpoints per phase, an honest assist, tuning verified by a bot (§17).\n28\t5. **The TV first.** A DualSense at ten feet. Big, single-focus screens. Keyboard and mouse feel native, not ported.\n29\t\n30\t### What to keep from the source, and what to change\n31\t\n32\t| Source trait | What we do |\n33\t|---|---|\n34\t| Radial tool menu on the analog stick | Keep. Left stick flick picks one of 8 tools in fixed compass positions |\n35\t| Tool rhythm: drain, gel, laser, forceps, suture, scalpel, syringe, defibrillator | Keep the genre's vocabulary. Add the **clamp** (beat-timed) and a **scanner**. Make the rules ours (§4) |\n36\t| A gift that bends time | Keep the fantasy with a new name and meaning: **the Hush**. Second doctor gets a different gift, **Sforzando** |\n37\t| Vitals, time limit, per-action grades, final rank | Keep the structure. New grade names and rank titles (§3) |\n38\t| Man-made parasite with escalating strains | Original parasite and strains: **REQUIEM** (§5) |\n39\t| A second doctor's story that recontextualizes the first | Keep the structure: Chapter 3 hands you a second surgeon who disagrees with the first. Original characters and plot (§7) |\n40\t| Melodramatic, soap-opera story | Keep the tone (§2.4) |\n41\t\n42\tFour things reviewers criticized in the source, which this design deliberately fixes:\n43\t\n44\t| Criticism | Fix |\n45\t|---|---|\n46\t| Star-drawing activation of the special was unreliable | The special is one reliable button (mirrored for the left thumb) |\n47\t| Difficulty spikes | Per-phase checkpoints, Bea's Notes assist, bot-verified timers (§3.8, §17) |\n48\t| Emotional disconnect from patients | Every patient has a chart with a personal gag and a one-line follow-up after the operation (§6) |\n49\t| Long text slows the pace | Scenes under 30 lines, always skippable, one-button fast-forward (§7) |\n50\t\n51\t### Originality rule\n52\t\n53\tBuild an original game. The title may riff on the source's name, as your brief asks, but nothing else carries over. Don't use the source's characters, disease, organizations, hospital, levels, art or music. Specifically never use: Derek Stiles, Angie Thompson, Naomi Kimishima, Caduceus, Delphi, GUILT or its strain names, Healing Touch, star drawing, Hope Hospital, the XS rank, or the grades Bad/Good/Cool.\n54\t\n55\t---\n56\t\n57\t## 2. World, cast and tone\n58\t\n59\t### 2.1 Setting\n60\t\n61\t**Port Calloway**, a rainy harbor city in the near future: ferries, cranes, neon pharmacy crosses. **St. Alder Memorial Hospital** is old, underfunded and proud. Its heart is **OR 3**. Beneath it lies **Ward Zero**, a secret emergency unit in a sealed polio ward, green tile and old pipes.\n62\t\n63\t### 2.2 Cast\n64\t\n65\tSilhouette first: each must be recognizable from outline alone at ten feet.\n66\t\n67\t| Character | Role | Pronouns | Look | Voice (§11) | How they talk |\n68\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n69\t| **Dr. Ren Halloran**, 27 | Protagonist, second-year surgical resident | he | Messy spiky dark-teal hair, big nervous eyes, teal scrubs, white coat, stethoscope | Mid-high, quick | Stammers and over-apologizes. In the OR: short, dead calm |\n70\t| **Bea Okonkwo**, 58 | Head nurse of OR 3 for 31 years | she | Marigold head-wrap, grey bun, round black glasses, deep-teal scrubs | Low, slow, warm | Calls everyone \"child\". Savage, devoted. **She never says \"good\"** until the last scene (§7) |\n71\t| **Dr. Augustin Bell**, 66 | Chief of Surgery | he | Bald, enormous white moustache, bow tie, wide build | Booming bass | Jovial aphorisms that turn out to be confessions |\n72\t| **Pip Adeyemi**, 24 | Pathology tech, weekend church organist | they | Two small hair buns, oversized yellow cardigan over scrubs | High, bubbly | Run-on sentences. Hears music in everything |\n73\t| **Hale Sorrento**, 45 | Director of Ward Zero | he | Slick hair, rectangular glasses, charcoal suit | Flat monotone | Deadpan bureaucratic menace |\n74\t| **Dr. Ines Varga**, 39 | Second playable surgeon, ex-Conservatory | she | Sharp black bob with a white streak, scar through left brow, black scrubs | Low, clipped | Never uses contractions. Rude truths |\n75\t| **Dr. Cordelia Voss, \"the Maestro\"**, 71 | Villain. Founder of St. Alder's cardiac unit, officially dead since a fire 17 years ago | she | Silver hair to the waist, tailcoat over a surgical gown, baton | Breathy, with organ-like harmonics | Musical metaphors, unhurried |\n76\t\n77\t### 2.3 REQUIEM and the Conservatory\n78\t\n79\t**REQUIEM** is the parasite: *Recombinant Endoparasitic Quiescent Immuno-Evasive Metazoan*. It is delivered with total scientific confidence and explains nothing. It nests in a host and entrains the heart: \"It's keeping time with her pulse.\"\n80\t\n81\t**The Conservatory** is Voss's cabal of former students. Before each outbreak the hospital receives an envelope with a page of sheet music, and the rhythm is the next victim's heartbeat. Each strain is a \"movement\" with a tempo marking as its name.\n82\t\n83\t**The Hush** is an operating-room legend: once in a generation, a surgeon hears the whole room go quiet and every heart in it. Bea has seen it once in 31 years, in Voss. The tragedy: Voss never stopped hearing it.\n84\t\n85\t### 2.4 Tone rules\n86\t\n87\t1. Everyone is sincere. The jokes come from characters, never from the game mocking itself.\n88\t2. Lines fit two lines of TV text (about 90 characters). A monologue is many short lines.\n89\t3. Medical jargon is poetry. The science is nonsense, delivered with total confidence.\n90\t4. \"Doctor\" as a vocative, constantly. \"Child\" from Bea.\n91\t5. One shout per scene: big comic lettering, speed lines, camera punch-in.\n92\t6. Every chapter ends on a sting.\n93\t7. Scenes run at most 30 lines and about 90 seconds.\n94\t\n95\tLines to tune your ear. Use these, and write the rest to match:\n96\t\n97\t- Bea: \"Thirty-one years in this room, child. You are the ninth boy they have told me is gifted.\"\n98\t- Ren: \"I-I've only ever done this on the simulator!\" / Bea: \"Then simulate faster.\"\n99\t- Bea: \"That is a shard of windshield, not a sacrament. Take it out.\"\n100\t- Bell: \"Surgery is the art of being wrong slowly enough to fix it.\"\n101\t- Bell: \"You have the best hands I have ever seen, Doctor, and the worst nerves.\"\n102\t- Pip: \"It's a heartbeat, it's literally a heartbeat, sixty-eight a minute with a skipped third, who writes that, who WRITES that?\"\n103\t- Sorrento: \"You have just pulled a biological weapon out of a dockworker, Doctor. Please wash your hands.\"\n104\t- Ines: \"Your hands are excellent. Your diagnosis is a eulogy.\"\n105\t- Ines: \"He waits for the silence. I do not wait.\"\n106\t- Voss: \"Every heart is a metronome, Doctor. I merely gave them something to play.\"\n107\t- Ren: \"She's a patient.\" (the last line of the climax, §7)\n108\t\n109\t---\n110\t\n111\t## 3. Core rules\n112\t\n113\tEverything here lives in the simulation (§15), with values from the tuning table (§13). `fu` means field unit.\n114\t\n115\t### 3.1 The field\n116\t\n117\tPlay happens on a flat logical plane. Field coordinates are `x` in [-1.35, 1.35], `y` in [-0.85, 0.85] (positive `y` is up the screen; world `z = -y`). The **instrument tray** zone sits to the right, centered at (1.62, 0.2), size 0.5 × 0.8. The tip is clamped to `x` in [-1.55, 1.9], `y` in [-0.95, 0.95].\n118\t\n119\tPhysics runs in this flat space: `y_world = 0` is the tissue surface. The view lifts everything by `heightAt(site, x, y)`, so Rapier never needs organ-shaped colliders. An operation has 1 to 3 **sites** (for example heart, lung, aorta). Each seat is at one site, and its tools touch only entities there. L1/R1 switch sites with a 0.35 s camera whip.\n120\t\n121\t### 3.2 The instrument tip\n122\t\n123\t- **Stick:** speed = `tipSpeed × |s|^stickCurve`, radial deadzone 0.12. `steadyFactor` applies while Steady is held.\n124\t- **Aim assist (pad and keyboard only):** near an active guide, and when the Use input is held on a cut or stitch, pull the tip toward the nearest guide point: `tip += (nearest - tip) × aimAssist × dt × 12`, within 0.12 fu.\n125\t- **Mouse:** absolute. The tip goes where the pointer is (project onto the field plane through the camera), limited to 4 fu/s while gripping an embedded object. Assist is a fixed 0.1, only near stitch gates. The OS cursor is hidden over the field and shown over HUD and menus.\n126\t\n127\t### 3.3 The beat clock\n128\t\n129\t`bpm = op.bpm + tempoPressure × (1 - vitals/100)`, smoothed over 1 s. A phase accumulator advances by `dt × timeScale × bpm/60`. Every wrap fires a `beat` event; 4 beats make a bar. The event drives tissue squeeze, ECG spike, HUD pulse, strain actions and rumble. The music follows the same `bpm` and aligns its quarter notes to `beat` (§11). Hush slows it; Fermata stops it.\n130\t\n131\t### 3.4 Vitals, clock, misses\n132\t\n133\t- **Vitals:** 0 to 100. Zero is a flatline and fails the operation. Hazards drain it (§4, §5). At 30 or below: danger state (red vignette, line boil, heartbeat rumble, Bea shouts).\n134\t- **Timer:** per operation. Zero fails the operation. It stays visible, and pulses under 30 s.\n135\t- **Misses:** a MISS adds to a counter. Reaching `missLimit` fails the operation (\"Malpractice\").\n136\t\n137\t### 3.5 Grades and score\n138\t\n139\tEach discrete action (a cut, a shard, a wound, a clamp, a kill) earns a grade, shown as comic lettering with an ink splat at the tip:\n140\t\n141\t| Grade | Base points | Composure |\n142\t|---|---|---|\n143\t| FLAWLESS | 300 | +10 |\n144\t| CLEAN | 150 | +5 |\n145\t| ROUGH | 50 | 0 |\n146\t| MISS | -100 | -10 |\n147\t\n148\tCombo: consecutive CLEAN-or-better multiplies points by `1 + 0.05 × combo`, up to ×2. MISS resets it. ROUGH holds it. On success add `vitals × 20 + seconds left × 5`, plus 1500 for no misses.\n149\t\n150\t### 3.6 Ranks\n151\t\n152\tFinal rank compares the score with the operation's `parScore` (data, set from the bot in §17):\n153\t\n154\t| Rank | Score vs par | Title on the stamp |\n155\t|---|---|---|\n156\t| S | at least 90% | Chief of Surgery |\n157\t| A | at least 75% | Attending |\n158\t| B | at least 55% | Resident |\n159\t| C | at least 35% | Intern |\n160\t| D | below 35% | Malpractice Suit Pending |\n161\t\n162\t### 3.7 Composure and the two gifts\n163\t\n164\tComposure is a 0 to 100 meter, gain scaled by `composureGain`. When full, the Special button fires the current doctor's gift, and the meter empties.\n165\t\n166\t- **The Hush (Ren):** a 0.4 s held breath with everything frozen, then for `hushDuration` seconds, strains, drains, timers and the beat run at `hushTimeScale` while your tip and tools run at full speed. An ink-wash expands from the tip: sumi-e monochrome, with red the only color left. Music low-passes down to almost nothing; only the slow heartbeat remains.\n167\t- **Sforzando (Ines):** a violent accented hit. All strains are stunned for `sforzandoStun` seconds and lose `sforzandoDamage` of their current HP, and tools work at `sforzandoToolMult` for `sforzandoDuration` seconds. Comic cut-in, one huge orchestral stab.\n168\t\n169\tIn Operation 7 both surgeons are in the room: D-pad Right (or R) swaps the lead doctor and therefore the gift. In Free Play, the chart offers either doctor.\n170\t\n171\t### 3.8 Difficulty, checkpoints, assist\n172\t\n173\t| | Easy (Intern) | Normal (Resident) | Hard (Attending) |\n174\t|---|---|---|---|\n175\t| Drains | ×0.6 | ×1 | ×1.35 |\n176\t| Timers | ×1.4 | ×1 | ×0.85 |\n177\t| Tolerances (`scalpelTol`, `stitchGateR`, `clampWindow`, `defibWindow`) | ×1.4 | ×1 | ×0.8 |\n178\t| `missLimit` | 12 | 8 | 5 |\n179\t| Strain HP | ×0.85 | ×1 | ×1.2 |\n180\t\n181\tNew Game asks once, in Bea's voice (\"How much do you hate yourself, child?\"), and every chart can change it.\n182\t\n183\t**Checkpoints:** every phase is a checkpoint. Failing offers *Retry phase*, *Restart operation*, *Quit*. Retrying a phase restores vitals to at least 60, the timer to at least 90 s, and the score to the checkpoint's. Phases must be self-contained: a phase's `spawn` lists everything it needs, so `skipTo(phase)` works for retry, dev mode and attract mode alike. Use `ifMissing: true` for what an earlier phase normally leaves behind, such as clamps.\n184\t\n185\t**Bea's Notes (assist):** after failing the same phase twice, Bea offers her notebook: 30% more time, 30% less drain, rank capped at B. It's optional, stated honestly, and shown on the report.\n186\t\n187\t**Hints:** 12 s without progress, Bea says the next step and the right wheel slot flashes. D-pad Down or H asks at any time, at no cost.\n188\t\n189\t### 3.9 Consults\n190\t\n191\tBefore Operations 5, 6 and 7, Ren and Ines each propose a plan, and the player chooses:\n192\t\n193\t| Plan | Effect |\n194\t|---|---|\n195\t| Ren's: careful | Timer +60 s, drains ×0.8, score ×0.9 |\n196\t| Ines's: aggressive | Timer -40 s, tools ×1.25, score ×1.15 |\n197\t\n198\t---\n199\t\n200\t## 4. Tools\n201\t\n202\t**The wheel never changes.** The left stick flick selects at once (deflection over 0.6, 8 sectors, with hysteresis). Keys 1 to 8 and the mouse wheel do the same, and the on-screen wheel is clickable.\n203\t\n204\t| Key | Dir | Tool | Used for |\n205\t|---|---|---|---|\n206\t| 1 | N | **Scalpel** | Trace incisions; sever tethers and chain links |\n207\t| 2 | NE | **Forceps** | Pull shards, tumors, stunned parasites; carry to tray |\n208\t| 3 | E | **Suction** | Drain pools |\n209\t| 4 | SE | **Gel** | Disinfect zones, heal scratches, clear spores, seal nests |\n210\t| 5 | S | **Suture** | Stitch wounds through gates |\n211\t| 6 | SW | **Laser** | Burn nodules and parasites, with analog power |\n212\t| 7 | W | **Scanner** | Reveal and mark hidden things; pin Tremolo |\n213\t| 8 | NW | **Clamp** | Stop spurting bleeders on the beat |\n214\t\n215\tOff the wheel: **Syringe** (one button, context-sensitive) and **Defibrillator** (event only).\n216\t\n217\t\"Use\" below means R2 (analog 0 to 1) or LMB/Cross (1.0). Using a tool on nothing is harmless, except the Scalpel and Laser on bare tissue. Wrong tool for a target: no penalty, Bea comments. Every rule below needs a Node test (§17).\n218\t\n219\t- **Scalpel.** Hold Use within 0.08 of a highlighted incision's start. Progress advances by projecting the tip onto the polyline while within `scalpelTol`.\n220\t  - Leaving tolerance while holding: a **nick**: MISS, vitals -3, small pool spawns. Progress is kept; re-enter to continue.\n221\t  - Speed over 1.8 fu/s caps the grade at ROUGH.\n222\t  - Grade by mean deviation: up to 0.35×tol FLAWLESS, up to 0.7×tol CLEAN, else ROUGH.\n223\t  - **Sever:** with Use held, the tip's path crossing a tether or chain link at 0.4 fu/s or more cuts it.\n224\t  - On bare tissue, moving with Use held: a nick every 0.4 s.\n225\t- **Forceps.** Use at 0.4 or more within 0.1 of a grabbable item grips it. Dropping below 0.4 lets go. Embedded items have an `axis` and an `embed` distance (0.15 to 0.6).\n226\t  - Pull within ±50° of the axis at or under `pullTol`: the item comes out, stretching on a Rapier spring, and pops free at full distance.\n227\t  - Pull faster: **tear**: MISS, vitals -4, item stays.\n228\t  - Wrong direction: it just resists.\n229\t  - Held items hang from the tip on a spring. Releasing over the tray deposits them, and they physically land and pile up. Releasing elsewhere: it falls, vitals -1, grab it again.\n230\t  - Grade by pull smoothness (speed variance).\n231\t- **Suction.** Hold Use: pools within radius 0.16 lose `suctionRate × use` volume per second, and droplets arc into the nozzle. Pool done at under 5%. CLEAN, or FLAWLESS if it never overflowed. A pool over its threshold covers guides and drains 0.4 vitals/s.\n232\t- **Gel.** Hold Use: paints a coverage grid (cell 0.04 fu) within `gelRadius`. A zone completes at 90%. Scratches heal when coated for 1 s. Spores clear on contact. Nest holes seal after 1 s. Grade against the zone's par time.\n233\t- **Suture.** A wound shows numbered gates, alternating sides. Hold Use and pass through them in order, within `stitchGateR`. Each gate pulls a stitch tight with a thread you can see (cosmetic rope, §9.7). Tearing more than 0.3 fu away with Use held: MISS. After the last gate, tie off with a quick flick (1.5 fu/s or more) or Alt within 1.5 s, or it auto-ties and can't be FLAWLESS. Grade by average distance from gate centers.\n234\t- **Laser.** Hold Use: a beam at the tip, DPS `laserDps × use` (use clamped to 0.3 to 1) on a target within 0.05. Off-target on tissue, after 0.25 s grace: scorch mark and `laserBurn × use` vitals/s. Six seconds of continuous fire forces a 1.5 s vent. FLAWLESS if no tissue burned on that target.\n235\t- **Scanner.** Hold Use: an x-ray circle of `scannerRadius` around the tip. Hidden things inside are visible; 0.6 s of dwell **marks** them (ping), and they stay marked. Hidden things can't be touched by other tools until marked. A dwell of 1 s on Tremolo pins it.\n236\t- **Clamp.** Use within 0.1 of a bleeder. A ring contracts onto each beat: within `clampWindow` of the beat FLAWLESS, within double CLEAN, otherwise ROUGH (it still works). Unclamped bleeders feed a pool and drain 1 vitals/s. Four clamps per operation. **Alt on a clamp removes it**, and clamps must be off before an operation ends, which the objective line states.\n237\t- **Syringe** (Square / D-pad Left / F). Over an injection site: hold 0.8 s to inject (FLAWLESS if finished on a beat). Anywhere else: 0.5 s plunge into the IV for `stabilizerHeal` vitals, then `stabilizerCooldown`. Unlimited doses.\n238\t- **Defibrillator** (event only). During an arrest, paddles appear. Hold both L2 and R2 (LMB+RMB, or J+K) for 1.2 s to charge while a ring grows; release both as the ring crosses the gold band (`defibWindow`). \"CLEAR!\" White flash, thump, the heart restarts. Too early or too late: weak shock, 2 s lost.\n239\t\n240\t---\n241\t\n242\t## 5. Strains\n243\t\n244\tEach strain is a musical notation symbol come to life. On first appearance: a comic **strain card** with a dictionary definition, and stave lines crawl across the organ in ink.\n245\t\n246\t| Strain | Card text | Look | Behavior | Counter | Music |\n247\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n248\t| **Adagio** | *adagio (adv.): slowly, with great expression. Also: it is eating her liver.* | Fat tube body with five yellow-green stave stripes; note-head on a thin stem | Crawls along a site path at 0.12 fu/s. Beat 1 of each bar leaves a scratch at its tail (max 3; 0.5 vitals/s each). Core glows on beats 3 to 4: Laser ×3 there. HP 100 | Laser to 0: stunned 5 s. Forceps it to the tray in time, or it wakes with 40 HP. Grande variant: HP 150, two tails, opens bleeders | Slow cello-like line |\n249\t| **Staccato** | *staccato (adj.): short, sharp, detached. Like her prognosis.* | Tiny black note-heads with flags popping out of holes | Holes spawn notes on off-beats. A note lives 2 beats, then bursts into a spore patch (-2 vitals, then 0.3 vitals/s until gelled). Hidden nests need the Scanner | Laser tap (0.25 s) or Forceps pinch (0.2 s). Gel 1 s (or 3 stitches) seals a nest | Pizzicato on the off-beats |\n250\t| **Tremolo** | *tremolo (n.): a rapid, nervous trembling. Yours, specifically.* | Pale moth with stave-striped wings, always shivering | Flees at 0.9 fu/s if the tip is within 0.6 fu moving faster than 0.45 fu/s. Scratches 1.5 vitals/s while fleeing. Freezes if you approach slowly | Steady in, Scanner 1 s to pin (3 s), Laser 40 HP. Three pins | Fast high strings |\n251\t| **Legato** | *legato (adj.): smoothly connected, without a break. It does not want a break.* | Bead chain joined by curved slur ribbons, on Rapier rope joints, head pinned | Must be cut **tail first**; the active link glows. Wrong link: two links re-heal, vitals -4. Cut pieces reattach after 8 beats unless carried to the tray. Every 8 beats it jerks and opens a bleeder | Scalpel sever, then Forceps to tray | Gliding portamento lead |\n252\t| **Crescendo** | *crescendo (n.): gradually getting louder. She is about to get very loud.* | A hairpin \"<\" wedge wrapped into a ring that widens | Size grows by `0.02 + 0.015 × live feeders` per second, from 0.2 to 1. Drain `1 + 6 × size` vitals/s. Four feeders (HP 40) regrow every 16 beats. At size 1: **Fortissimo**: -20 vitals, 2 bleeders, size resets to 0.6 | Kill all feeders: the ring shrinks and shows a circular cut line. Scalpel the circle, Forceps it out | One new voice every bar |\n253\t| **Fermata** | *fermata (n.): hold. Hold until the conductor releases you. The conductor is not coming.* | The fermata sign as a giant eye: an arc and a dot, opening on the heart | Stops the heart: music cuts to silence, ECG flat, 8 s arrest clock, vitals hold at 15. While the heart is stopped the eye is exposed | Laser the eye (HP 60, then 80) during the pause, then Defib before the clock ends. Between arrests: inject 3 sites. Finally Forceps | One held organ chord |\n254\t| **Coda** | *coda (n.): the tail. The part that ends everything.* | Coda sign (circle and cross) as a core, two long baton arms on Rapier chains, a tailcoat membrane | **Beat 1 Downbeat:** ring telegraphs one beat early; the baton slams, -6 vitals and a scratch, unless you Laser the baton 0.5 s during the wind-up. **Beat 3 Cue:** spawns 2 notes or a small Adagio. **Every 4th bar a rest:** the core opens for 2 beats, Laser ×3. At 66% and 33% of HP 300 it moves to another site and its arms regrow; sever the arms to reach it. At 0: long Forceps pull (embed 0.6) | All of it | All the motifs in counterpoint, with the Dies Irae (§11) on organ |\n255\t\n256\t---\n257\t\n258\t## 6. Operations\n259\t\n260\tAbout 40 to 55 minutes in total. Timers are for Normal. **Every operation also has a chart:** patient name, age, job, one personal gag, and a one-line follow-up shown on the report. Phases are data (§15.3).\n261\t\n262\t### Op 1: Broken Glass (Chapter 1, Ren). Forearm. Timer 4:00, 72 bpm\n263\t*Desmond Pell, 52, taxi driver. \"Has never once used a turn signal.\" Follow-up: \"Discharged Tuesday. Was seen signaling, once, leaving the car park.\"*\n264\tTeaches: tip, wheel, Gel, Forceps, Suction, Syringe, Scanner, Suture.\n265\t1. **Clean:** one gel zone (1.2 × 0.5).\n266\t2. **Pull the glass:** 4 shards (embed 0.18 to 0.3). Each pull leaves a small pool (volume 0.4). Done when shards are on the tray and pools are drained.\n267\t3. **Vitals dip:** scripted -25. Bea teaches the Syringe. Can't fail here.\n268\t4. **The missing shard:** Bea: \"Count them, child.\" Scanner finds a deep shard. Forceps it.\n269\t5. **Stitch:** two lacerations (6 and 8 gates).\n270\t6. **Finish:** gel zone over the stitches.\n271\t\n272\t### Op 2: Growth Spurt (Chapter 1, Ren). Abdomen skin, then bowel. Timer 5:30, 76 bpm\n273\t*Hana Ito, 38, crane operator. \"Lifts forty tons for a living. Fainted at the consent form.\" Follow-up: \"Back on the cranes. Asks after you. Has asked you to stop visiting.\"*\n274\tTeaches: Scalpel, Clamp, Laser, sever, the Hush.\n275\t1. **Open:** gel zone, one long incision (1.4 fu). Ink wipe to the bowel site.\n276\t2. **Bleed:** 2 bleeders and rising pools. Clamp and Suction.\n277\t3. **Nodules:** 5 (HP 30), one hidden. Laser, Scanner.\n278\t4. **The tumor:** sever 3 tethers. Forceps it out (embed 0.4) and carry it to the tray.\n279\t5. **The crash:** the tumor twitches on the tray. Vitals forced to 35, drain 3/s, a rupture wound (12 gates) opens, Composure is set to 100. Bea screams. The Hush prompt appears. The moment it fires: silence, ink wash, the first time. Suture the rupture.\n280\t6. **Close:** take clamps off, close incision (10 gates), gel.\n281\t\n282\t### Op 3: Adagio (Chapter 2, Ren). Liver. Timer 5:30, 80 bpm\n283\t*Theodora Lund, 61, cellist, Harbor Orchestra. \"Refuses anesthesia until she has finished a sentence about Brahms.\" Follow-up: \"Performed in April. Dedicated the slow movement to 'the nervous one.'\"*\n284\t1. **Open:** gel, short incision.\n285\t2. **Look closer:** Scanner sweep reveals stave lines and 3 nests. Adagio emerges, with strain card.\n286\t3. **Adagio:** Laser it down, Forceps it out.\n287\t4. **Eggs:** 4 hidden (Scanner, Laser HP 20) hatching at bar 16 into small Adagios.\n288\t5. **Adagio Grande:** HP 150, two tails, opens bleeders. Clamp them.\n289\t6. **Close:** clamps off, suture 2 lesions, gel.\n290\t\n291\t### Op 4: Staccato (Chapter 2, Ren). Bowel, then stomach. Timer 6:00, 84 bpm\n292\t*Ruben Castellano, 47, ferry captain. \"Has not missed a crossing in nineteen years. Is asking whether this will take long.\" Follow-up: \"Missed two crossings. Sent flowers to the ferry.\"*\n293\t1. **Open:** gel, incision.\n294\t2. **Wave 1:** 8 notes from 3 open holes.\n295\t3. **Find the nests:** Scanner, 4 hidden holes. Seal them.\n296\t4. **Waves 2 and 3:** 12 and 16 notes, faster.\n297\t5. **Tremolo:** first appears here. Bea: \"Slow hands, child.\" Three pins.\n298\t6. **Close:** suture, gel.\n299\t\n300\t### Op 5: Legato (Chapter 3, **Ines**). Brain. Timer 6:00, 70 bpm. Consult\n301\t*Nadia Bloom, 29, school music teacher. \"Her class has sent a card signed 'Please do not miss.'\" Follow-up: \"Back with Class 4. They have sent a second card.\"*\n302\tTeaches: Sforzando, the Consult. Scalpel tolerance ×0.75 throughout: this is a brain.\n303\t1. **Consult,** then **membrane:** two precise arcs.\n304\t2. **Legato:** 12 links, tail first. Forceps each piece away.\n305\t3. **Thrash:** at link 6 it speeds up. Composure set to 100. Ines: \"Now you will see how I work.\" Sforzando prompt.\n306\t4. **Second chain:** 8 links with two tails.\n307\t5. **Repair:** suture the vessel (tight gates), clamps off, gel.\n308\t\n309\t### Op 6: Fermata (Chapter 3, Ren, with Ines on comms). Heart. Timer 7:00, 88 bpm. Consult\n310\t*Dr. Augustin Bell, 66, Chief of Surgery. \"Has told every surgeon in this hospital to quit smoking. Smokes.\" Follow-up: \"Under arrest. Recovering. Has quit.\"*\n311\t1. **Consult,** then **open:** long incision, gel.\n312\t2. **Crescendo:** feeders, Fortissimo threat, cut out the ring.\n313\t3. **First arrest:** the eye opens, silence. Defib tutorial, no Laser needed.\n314\t4. **Antidote:** 3 injection sites, plus bleeders.\n315\t5. **Second arrest:** Laser the eye (HP 60), then Defib.\n316\t6. **Third arrest:** Laser the eye (HP 80), Defib, then Forceps the eye out.\n317\t7. **Close:** clamps off, suture, gel.\n318\t\n319\t### Op 7: Coda (Chapter 4, Ren and Ines). Heart, lung, aorta. Timer 9:30, 92 bpm. Consult\n320\t*Beatrice Okonkwo, 58, Head Nurse. The chart is in her own handwriting: \"Do not be dramatic about this.\" Follow-up: \"Discharged herself. Is back in OR 3. Has opinions.\"*\n321\tBea is unconscious, so **Pip reads her notebook aloud** as the in-operation voice. This is the emotional core: every bark is her words in someone else's voice (\"'Suction before you can't see.' She underlined that twice.\").\n322\t1. **Exposition (heart):** Adagio and Staccato together.\n323\t2. **Development (lung, breathing field):** Tremolo and a Legato chain. Bleeders open back at the heart: site-switch alarm, teaching L1/R1.\n324\t3. **Recapitulation (aorta):** Crescendo, then a Fermata arrest. Every monitor in the hospital audibly falls into time with hers.\n325\t4. **Coda (moves across all three sites):** the Conductor (§5).\n326\t5. **Closing:** wounds on all three sites, clamps off.\n327\t6. **The last stop:** her heart stops, no warning and no hint. Composure refills. Hush, total silence, one Defib. Then her ECG, alone, and the music returns on the downbeat.\n328\t\n329\t### Last Patient (Chapter 4 ending, Ren). Heart. No HUD, no timer, can't fail\n330\t*Dr. Cordelia Voss, 71. Chart: \"Deceased,\" with a date 17 years ago, crossed out by Ren.*\n331\tForty seconds. Rain. One clamp, one Defib. Report: no rank, only: \"Tonight's patient survived.\"\n332\t\n333\t### Grand Rounds (co-op, Free Play and hub rounds). Liver, bowel, heart. Timer 5:00\n334\t*Ngaio Reeve, 55, harbor master. \"Ward Zero has only this to say: everyone scrub in.\"*\n335\tEach movement overlaps the last, so a team can split up. Counts scale by `1 + 0.5 × (players - 1)`, strain HP by `1 + 0.25 × (players - 1)`.\n336\t1. **0:00, Open up:** 2 incisions (liver), 4 shards (bowel), gel zones, 2 bleeders.\n337\t2. **1:00, Adagio and Staccato:** Adagio on the liver, a nest on the bowel.\n338\t3. **2:15, Legato and Crescendo:** a chain on the bowel, a ring on the heart.\n339\t4. **3:30, Fermata:** one arrest. **Any one surgeon's shock counts**; simultaneous shocks earn a bonus. Injection sites at every site.\n340\t5. **4:15, Close:** wounds everywhere. Done before the timer: win.\n341\t\n342\t---\n343\t\n344\t## 7. Story\n345\t\n346\tDelivered as short scenes on a 3D stage (§9.5). Hold Cross/Space to fast-forward; a skip prompt skips the scene. Write 250 to 350 lines in total, in `src/data/story/`. The beats and must-have lines below are fixed; the rest is yours. Chapter names: **Scrub In**, **First Movement**, **Dissent**, **Coda**.\n347\t\n348\t| # | When | Where | Beats and must-have lines |\n349\t|---|---|---|---|\n350\t| S0 | New game | OR 3 at night, empty | An envelope slides under the door. The monitor switches itself on and beeps, in time, with no patient. A silhouette lifts a baton. Title sting. 20 s |\n351\t| S1 | Before Op 1 | Corridor, OR 3 | Flood traffic has stranded the attending. Ren is all there is. \"Thirty-one years in this room, child…\" \"Then simulate faster.\" |\n352\t| S2 | After Op 1 | Bell's office | Bell's praise and warning: \"the best hands… the worst nerves.\" |\n353\t| S3 | Before Op 2 | OR 3 gallery | The board watches. Routine tumor. Ren throws up, apologizes to the bucket |\n354\t| S4 | After Op 2 | OR 3, tray | Ren: \"Everything went quiet.\" Bea goes very still: \"I have heard of that once.\" The tumor twitches on the tray. In rhythm. Sting: \"Tumors do not keep time, child.\" |\n355\t| S5 | Before Op 3 | Pip's pathology lab | Envelope: sheet music addressed to \"The Surgeon on Call\". Pip plays it on a keyboard: \"It's a heartbeat…\" Ambulance: a cellist |\n356\t| S6 | After Op 3 | Ward Zero | Sorrento: \"Please wash your hands.\" REQUIEM, spelled out in full, with a straight face. The Conservatory. Ren is drafted |\n357\t| S7 | Before Op 4 | Ambulance bay, rain | Second score. A ferry captain |\n358\t| S8 | After Op 4 | OR 3 | The OR phone rings. A breathy voice: \"Bravo, Doctor. You play by ear. How delightful.\" Bea drops a full instrument tray. Sting |\n359\t| S9 | Before Op 5 | Ward Zero, then OR 3 | Ines arrives under guard. \"Your hands are excellent. Your diagnosis is a eulogy.\" She operates; you play her. First Consult |\n360\t| S10 | After Op 5 | Hospital roof, rain | Ines explains: she studied under the Maestro, who had the Hush. \"At the end, Doctor, you cannot turn it off.\" \"He waits for the silence. I do not wait.\" |\n361\t| S11 | Before Op 6 | Boardroom | Bell collapses mid-aphorism. The envelope's dedication: \"For Augustin, who signed.\" Consult |\n362\t| S12 | After Op 6 | Recovery room | Bell confesses: he signed Voss's death certificate after the Ward Zero fire, knowing she lived, and funded St. Alder with her research. \"Every heart I mended in this building, I mended with her money.\" Bea: \"I handed her the knife for eleven years. I would know that silence anywhere.\" |\n363\t| S13 | Before Op 7 | OR 3 | Final envelope. Pip plays it and slows down: \"I know this rhythm. It has a murmur. Ms. Okonkwo, you've got a—\" Bea, turning, mid-sentence: \"Forceps, ch—\" She falls. Ines: \"You cannot operate. You love her.\" Ren: \"Then you had better hand me the instruments.\" Pip, shaking, picks up the notebook. Consult |\n364\t| S14 | After Op 7 | Recovery, dawn | Bea wakes. Long silence. \"You held the forceps wrong, child.\" |\n365\t| S15 | Then | OR 3 gallery | Voss, conducting behind the glass, falters: her own heart is the first host. Sorrento: \"Let her go, Doctor.\" Ren: \"She's a patient.\" Leads into Last Patient |\n366\t| S16 | After | St. Alder, morning | Patients' cameos. Pip in student-nurse scrubs. Bea, with a cane, watches Ren close a wound and, for the only time in the game: **\"Good hands, child.\"** Credits roll over attract footage. Post-credits: a new envelope, marked *Opus 2* |\n367\t\n368\t**Bea's barks.** Write 60 or so short in-operation lines keyed by event, each with an expression: `start, nextPhase, toolHint(tool), idle, vitalsLow, vitalsCritical, miss, streak, bleeding, overflow, clampReminder, strainIntro(strain), hushReady, timerLow, success, fail`. A taste:\n369\t\n370\t- `vitalsLow`: \"Pressure's falling, child. Stabilizer.\"\n371\t- `miss`: \"That was not on the line.\" / \"I saw that. God saw that.\"\n372\t- `streak`: \"…Adequate.\" (her highest praise)\n373\t- `bleeding`: \"You are swimming in it. Suction.\"\n374\t- `clampReminder`: \"Your clamps are still in. Would you like to sign for them?\"\n375\t- `timerLow`: \"Clock, child.\"\n376\t- `fail`: \"Call it. Then go and tell the family. No. You. That is the job.\"\n377\t\n378\t**Lint:** a test fails if any Bea line contains \"good\" outside S16. In Op 5 Bea still calls the barks and trades shots with Ines. In Op 6 Ines interjects over comms. In Op 7 every bark comes from Pip reading the notebook.\n379\t\n380\t---\n381\t\n382\t## 8. Controls and screens\n383\t\n384\t### 8.1 Operating controls\n385\t\n386\tDesign order: DualSense, then keyboard and mouse, then the Xbox glyph option. Same game, same screens.\n387\t\n388\t| Action | DualSense | Xbox glyphs | Keyboard and mouse |\n389\t|---|---|---|---|\n390\t| Move the tip | Right stick | RS | Mouse. Keyboard-only: WASD or arrows |\n391\t| Pick a tool | Left stick flick | LS | 1 to 8, mouse wheel, click the wheel |\n392\t| Use (analog) | R2 (Cross = full) | RT (A) | LMB (J or Enter on keyboard only) |\n393\t| Steady | L2 | LT | Shift |\n394\t| Alt: drop, remove clamp, tie off | Circle | B | RMB (K) |\n395\t| Syringe | Square or D-pad Left | X or D-pad Left | F |\n396\t| Special | Triangle or D-pad Up | Y or D-pad Up | Space |\n397\t| Site back / next | L1 / R1 | LB / RB | Q / E |\n398\t| Swap lead doctor (Op 7) | D-pad Right | D-pad Right | R |\n399\t| Ask Bea | D-pad Down | D-pad Down | H |\n400\t| Recenter tip | L3 | LS click | C |\n401\t| Pause | Options | Menu | Esc |\n402\t\n403\tThe D-pad mirrors exist so a left thumb can fire Special and Syringe without taking the right thumb off the stick.\n404\t\n405\t**Pads:** accept any pad the browser reports with `mapping === 'standard'`. Read `axes` and `buttons[i].value` directly. If the active pad disconnects mid-operation, pause.\n406\t\n407\t**Rumble:** `vibrationActuator.playEffect('dual-rumble', …)` if present, with a Settings toggle. Heartbeat double-pulse at low vitals, a tick per stitch, a click for clamps, a slam for Defib. Guard against a missing actuator; it can't be tested here beyond the guard.\n408\t\n409\t### 8.2 Menu controls\n410\t\n411\tPad: D-pad or left stick, Cross confirm, Circle back, L1/R1 pages. Keyboard: arrows or WASD, Enter or Space confirm, Esc or Backspace back, Q/E pages. Mouse: hover moves the focus (there's always exactly one focused item), click confirms, wheel scrolls, RMB backs out. In story scenes a click advances the line. In an operation, the wheel's slots and the site tabs are clickable, and the parallax follows the pointer.\n412\t\n413\t### 8.3 Prompts\n414\t\n415\tAll on-screen prompts and any text naming a control use action tokens (`{use}`, `{steady}`, `{special}`), rendered by `src/input/glyphs.js` from the input used last:\n416\t\n417\t- **Pad, PlayStation (default):** drawn SVG shapes for ✕ ○ □ △ in their usual colors; L1/R1/L2/R2 pills; stick icons.\n418\t- **Pad, Xbox** (Settings option): A B X Y in their usual colors; LB/RB/LT/RT.\n419\t- **Keyboard and mouse:** keycaps and mouse-button icons.\n420\t\n421\tDevice switching: any key press means keyboard; mouse movement over 4 px or a click means mouse; a pad button or stick past deadzone means pad. Set `data-prompt-set` on `<html>` so tests can read it. Poll pads every frame; don't rely on `gamepadconnected`.\n422\t\n423\t### 8.4 Ten-foot rules (tests can't judge these; check at 1920×1080)\n424\t\n425\t- Scale all UI with `html { font-size: calc(100vh / 1080 * 16) }` and rem units. It must still hold together in a 640×400 hub frame.\n426\t- Minimum text at 1080p: 34 px anywhere, 44 px dialogue, 40 px objective, 56 px or more for key numbers.\n427\t- 5% safe margin. At most 7 items on a screen. One focus. No hover-only information. No tables outside dev mode.\n428\t- Nothing web-like: no scrollbars, no small links, no dense data screens.\n429\t\n430\t### 8.5 The operating HUD\n431\t\n432\tThe field fills the center, and the HUD holds the edges.\n433\t\n434\t| Where | What |\n435\t|---|---|\n436\t| Top center | Big ECG trace (2D canvas) sweeping on the beat clock, vitals number, bpm |\n437\t| Top left | Objective line and icons of the tool(s) needed |\n438\t| Top right | Timer, score, combo. Co-op: seat chips (name in seat color, current tool) |\n439\t| Bottom left | The tool wheel, large and always visible. Next tool's slot flashes after a hint |\n440\t| Bottom center | Bea's portrait (live 3D bust, §9.6) and her bark in a comic bubble |\n441\t| Bottom right | Composure gauge, Syringe cooldown, button prompts for the current context |\n442\t| Bottom center, above portrait | Site tabs (only with more than one site), with an alarm icon when another site is in trouble |\n443\t\n444\t### 8.6 Screens\n445\t\n446\t| Screen | Contents |\n447\t|---|---|\n448\t| Boot | Instant HTML \"Scrubbing in…\" with a pure-CSS ECG, before the heavy script runs |\n449\t| Touch notice | Touch-only devices. See below |\n450\t| Title | 3D lamp swings on over OR 3 and a heartbeat; logo with an ECG line through it; \"Press any button\" |\n451\t| Main Menu | Story (Continue / New), Free Play, Grand Rounds, Settings, About & Credits. At most 5 big items |\n452\t| Story | §9.5 |\n453\t| Chart | Before each operation: patient bust, name, job, gag, difficulty, Consult when due, big Begin |\n454\t| Operation | §8.5 |\n455\t| Pause | Resume, Retry Phase, Restart Operation, Settings, Dev Mode, Quit to Menu. Opens by itself when the tab is hidden (outside sessions) |\n456\t| Fail | FLATLINE or TIME or MALPRACTICE in huge lettering, Bea's line, Retry Phase / Restart / Quit, Bea's Notes offer when due |\n457\t| Report | A 3D clipboard. Only four stats (Time, Vitals, Precision, Best Combo), the rank stamp slamming down, the patient's follow-up, Continue |\n458\t| Free Play | Chart Board: patient cards with best rank stamps. A card appears once its operation is reached in Story; dev mode unlocks all. Choose difficulty and doctor |\n459\t| Grand Rounds lobby | Four seat cards. \"Press {confirm} to join\" (§14.5) |\n460\t| Settings | Three pages. **Audio:** Music (default 10%), Effects, Voices. **Controls:** Glyphs (PlayStation/Xbox), Rumble, Aim Assist, Swap Sticks, Tip Speed. **Display:** Fullscreen, Screen Shake, Line Boil, Text Speed |\n461\t| About & Credits | §19 |\n462\t| Credits roll | After S16, skippable, over attract footage |\n463\t| Session card, countdown | §14.4 |\n464\t\n465\t**Touch-only devices.** Detect with `matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches === false` and no pad seen. Don't use `navigator.maxTouchPoints`: it reported 0 on the WebKit iPhone and iPad profiles here. Show **\"Controller or keyboard required\"** and a **Settings** button, and no touch controls of any kind. Settings must work by tap on this path. Attract mode still starts after 10 s idle, behind the notice, and a tap returns to it. A key press or pad press leaves the notice for the normal Title.\n466\t\n467\t---\n468\t\n469\t## 9. Art direction\n470\t\n471\t**Native base:** Three.js `MeshToonNodeMaterial` with a stepped ramp, real lights and shadows, then the ink filter in §16.5. All geometry is made in code. Use the engine's stock generators freely (`RoundedBoxGeometry`, `LatheGeometry`, `TubeGeometry`, `ExtrudeGeometry`, and so on) for generic props. Make new art for what makes the game feel like its source: organs, instruments, strains, characters.\n472\t\n473\tThe look is **a comic-book medical drama**: fat ink lines with a hand-drawn wobble, manga tone in the shadows, glossy candy-colored organs with hard white glints. Stylized and bright, T-rated: blood is glossy crimson paint, never realistic.\n474\t\n475\t### 9.1 Palette\n476\t\n477\t| Use | Color |\n478\t|---|---|\n479\t| Drape and UI teal / OR background | `#17707A` / `#0B2529` |\n480\t| UI paper / ink / marigold / alarm | `#F4F1E8` / `#12081A` / `#F2B134` / `#E8443A` |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f4f41ba1-e4d3-4847-8e93-cb8d40d15190","uuid":"98ab2935-b5e9-490d-9c9b-7794f8ccbbfb","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:15:14.740Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211920__xe6d801/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Game Plan: a surgical action game inspired by *Trauma Center: Second Opinion*\n\nYou are building this game from this document, without its author. Your brief comes first; this plan turns the brief's open choices into decisions. Where the plan is silent, choose whatever serves the pillars in §1. Don't stop to ask.\n\n- **Title:** yours. Pick it the way your brief says and keep it in one file, `src/config/identity.js`. No other source file, line of dialogue or asset may contain it. The logo must fit any title length.\n- **File and folder names:** your brief names the dev folder, the final HTML file and the exit interview file. This plan never does. Every path below is relative to the dev folder.\n- **Verified recipes:** everything in §16 was run on this computer on 2026-11-04 with the exact versions named. If a recipe disagrees with your instincts, trust the recipe.\n- **Contents:** §1 the game · §2 world and cast · §3 core rules · §4 tools · §5 strains · §6 operations · §7 story · §8 controls and screens · §9 art · §10 animation · §11 audio · §12 attract mode · §13 dev mode · §14 multiplayer · §15 architecture · §16 verified recipes and traps · §17 testing · §18 build order · §19 About screen, exit interview, catalogue · §20 brief coverage checklist\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game\n\n**Genre:** surgical action (arcade operation game) with a visual-novel story between operations.\n\n**Perspective:** first-person surgeon's-eye view of a 3D operative field, from a fixed, tilted camera (§9.2). Story scenes are staged third-person two-shots of 3D character busts.\n\n**Pitch:** a rain-soaked medical soap opera where a nervous young surgeon fights an engineered parasite that keeps time with the human heart. Each operation is 3 to 7 minutes of tool-switching panic: cut, pull, drain, burn, stitch, while the patient's heart races, the music speeds up with it, and a head nurse tells you exactly what she thinks of your hands. Then a rank stamp slams down on the chart, and the next envelope of sheet music arrives.\n\n**The hook, which is ours and not the source's: the heartbeat is the clock.** One beat clock drives the ECG, the tissue's squeeze, the music's tempo, the parasites' attacks and the controller's rumble. As vitals fall, the heart races, so everything speeds up. Every parasite is a piece of music, drawn in musical notation, and it plays the patient's heart like an instrument.\n\n### Pillars\n\n1. **Every second is a heartbeat.** If something moves, sounds or attacks, it does so on the beat.\n2. **Hands you can feel.** Analog trigger pressure, physical pulls with real tension, the pop, the arc to the tray, the clink. Objects you remove stay piled on the tray as trophies.\n3. **Soap-opera sincerity.** Earnest, melodramatic, medically absurd, and never winking.\n4. **One more try.** Hard, but never a spike: checkpoints per phase, an honest assist, tuning verified by a bot (§17).\n5. **The TV first.** A DualSense at ten feet. Big, single-focus screens. Keyboard and mouse feel native, not ported.\n\n### What to keep from the source, and what to change\n\n| Source trait | What we do |\n|---|---|\n| Radial tool menu on the analog stick | Keep. Left stick flick picks one of 8 tools in fixed compass positions |\n| Tool rhythm: drain, gel, laser, forceps, suture, scalpel, syringe, defibrillator | Keep the genre's vocabulary. Add the **clamp** (beat-timed) and a **scanner**. Make the rules ours (§4) |\n| A gift that bends time | Keep the fantasy with a new name and meaning: **the Hush**. Second doctor gets a different gift, **Sforzando** |\n| Vitals, time limit, per-action grades, final rank | Keep the structure. New grade names and rank titles (§3) |\n| Man-made parasite with escalating strains | Original parasite and strains: **REQUIEM** (§5) |\n| A second doctor's story that recontextualizes the first | Keep the structure: Chapter 3 hands you a second surgeon who disagrees with the first. Original characters and plot (§7) |\n| Melodramatic, soap-opera story | Keep the tone (§2.4) |\n\nFour things reviewers criticized in the source, which this design deliberately fixes:\n\n| Criticism | Fix |\n|---|---|\n| Star-drawing activation of the special was unreliable | The special is one reliable button (mirrored for the left thumb) |\n| Difficulty spikes | Per-phase checkpoints, Bea's Notes assist, bot-verified timers (§3.8, §17) |\n| Emotional disconnect from patients | Every patient has a chart with a personal gag and a one-line follow-up after the operation (§6) |\n| Long text slows the pace | Scenes under 30 lines, always skippable, one-button fast-forward (§7) |\n\n### Originality rule\n\nBuild an original game. The title may riff on the source's name, as your brief asks, but nothing else carries over. Don't use the source's characters, disease, organizations, hospital, levels, art or music. Specifically never use: Derek Stiles, Angie Thompson, Naomi Kimishima, Caduceus, Delphi, GUILT or its strain names, Healing Touch, star drawing, Hope Hospital, the XS rank, or the grades Bad/Good/Cool.\n\n---\n\n## 2. World, cast and tone\n\n### 2.1 Setting\n\n**Port Calloway**, a rainy harbor city in the near future: ferries, cranes, neon pharmacy crosses. **St. Alder Memorial Hospital** is old, underfunded and proud. Its heart is **OR 3**. Beneath it lies **Ward Zero**, a secret emergency unit in a sealed polio ward, green tile and old pipes.\n\n### 2.2 Cast\n\nSilhouette first: each must be recognizable from outline alone at ten feet.\n\n| Character | Role | Pronouns | Look | Voice (§11) | How they talk |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Dr. Ren Halloran**, 27 | Protagonist, second-year surgical resident | he | Messy spiky dark-teal hair, big nervous eyes, teal scrubs, white coat, stethoscope | Mid-high, quick | Stammers and over-apologizes. In the OR: short, dead calm |\n| **Bea Okonkwo**, 58 | Head nurse of OR 3 for 31 years | she | Marigold head-wrap, grey bun, round black glasses, deep-teal scrubs | Low, slow, warm | Calls everyone \"child\". Savage, devoted. **She never says \"good\"** until the last scene (§7) |\n| **Dr. Augustin Bell**, 66 | Chief of Surgery | he | Bald, enormous white moustache, bow tie, wide build | Booming bass | Jovial aphorisms that turn out to be confessions |\n| **Pip Adeyemi**, 24 | Pathology tech, weekend church organist | they | Two small hair buns, oversized yellow cardigan over scrubs | High, bubbly | Run-on sentences. Hears music in everything |\n| **Hale Sorrento**, 45 | Director of Ward Zero | he | Slick hair, rectangular glasses, charcoal suit | Flat monotone | Deadpan bureaucratic menace |\n| **Dr. Ines Varga**, 39 | Second playable surgeon, ex-Conservatory | she | Sharp black bob with a white streak, scar through left brow, black scrubs | Low, clipped | Never uses contractions. Rude truths |\n| **Dr. Cordelia Voss, \"the Maestro\"**, 71 | Villain. Founder of St. Alder's cardiac unit, officially dead since a fire 17 years ago | she | Silver hair to the waist, tailcoat over a surgical gown, baton | Breathy, with organ-like harmonics | Musical metaphors, unhurried |\n\n### 2.3 REQUIEM and the Conservatory\n\n**REQUIEM** is the parasite: *Recombinant Endoparasitic Quiescent Immuno-Evasive Metazoan*. It is delivered with total scientific confidence and explains nothing. It nests in a host and entrains the heart: \"It's keeping time with her pulse.\"\n\n**The Conservatory** is Voss's cabal of former students. Before each outbreak the hospital receives an envelope with a page of sheet music, and the rhythm is the next victim's heartbeat. Each strain is a \"movement\" with a tempo marking as its name.\n\n**The Hush** is an operating-room legend: once in a generation, a surgeon hears the whole room go quiet and every heart in it. Bea has seen it once in 31 years, in Voss. The tragedy: Voss never stopped hearing it.\n\n### 2.4 Tone rules\n\n1. Everyone is sincere. The jokes come from characters, never from the game mocking itself.\n2. Lines fit two lines of TV text (about 90 characters). A monologue is many short lines.\n3. Medical jargon is poetry. The science is nonsense, delivered with total confidence.\n4. \"Doctor\" as a vocative, constantly. \"Child\" from Bea.\n5. One shout per scene: big comic lettering, speed lines, camera punch-in.\n6. Every chapter ends on a sting.\n7. Scenes run at most 30 lines and about 90 seconds.\n\nLines to tune your ear. Use these, and write the rest to match:\n\n- Bea: \"Thirty-one years in this room, child. You are the ninth boy they have told me is gifted.\"\n- Ren: \"I-I've only ever done this on the simulator!\" / Bea: \"Then simulate faster.\"\n- Bea: \"That is a shard of windshield, not a sacrament. Take it out.\"\n- Bell: \"Surgery is the art of being wrong slowly enough to fix it.\"\n- Bell: \"You have the best hands I have ever seen, Doctor, and the worst nerves.\"\n- Pip: \"It's a heartbeat, it's literally a heartbeat, sixty-eight a minute with a skipped third, who writes that, who WRITES that?\"\n- Sorrento: \"You have just pulled a biological weapon out of a dockworker, Doctor. Please wash your hands.\"\n- Ines: \"Your hands are excellent. Your diagnosis is a eulogy.\"\n- Ines: \"He waits for the silence. I do not wait.\"\n- Voss: \"Every heart is a metronome, Doctor. I merely gave them something to play.\"\n- Ren: \"She's a patient.\" (the last line of the climax, §7)\n\n---\n\n## 3. Core rules\n\nEverything here lives in the simulation (§15), with values from the tuning table (§13). `fu` means field unit.\n\n### 3.1 The field\n\nPlay happens on a flat logical plane. Field coordinates are `x` in [-1.35, 1.35], `y` in [-0.85, 0.85] (positive `y` is up the screen; world `z = -y`). The **instrument tray** zone sits to the right, centered at (1.62, 0.2), size 0.5 × 0.8. The tip is clamped to `x` in [-1.55, 1.9], `y` in [-0.95, 0.95].\n\nPhysics runs in this flat space: `y_world = 0` is the tissue surface. The view lifts everything by `heightAt(site, x, y)`, so Rapier never needs organ-shaped colliders. An operation has 1 to 3 **sites** (for example heart, lung, aorta). Each seat is at one site, and its tools touch only entities there. L1/R1 switch sites with a 0.35 s camera whip.\n\n### 3.2 The instrument tip\n\n- **Stick:** speed = `tipSpeed × |s|^stickCurve`, radial deadzone 0.12. `steadyFactor` applies while Steady is held.\n- **Aim assist (pad and keyboard only):** near an active guide, and when the Use input is held on a cut or stitch, pull the tip toward the nearest guide point: `tip += (nearest - tip) × aimAssist × dt × 12`, within 0.12 fu.\n- **Mouse:** absolute. The tip goes where the pointer is (project onto the field plane through the camera), limited to 4 fu/s while gripping an embedded object. Assist is a fixed 0.1, only near stitch gates. The OS cursor is hidden over the field and shown over HUD and menus.\n\n### 3.3 The beat clock\n\n`bpm = op.bpm + tempoPressure × (1 - vitals/100)`, smoothed over 1 s. A phase accumulator advances by `dt × timeScale × bpm/60`. Every wrap fires a `beat` event; 4 beats make a bar. The event drives tissue squeeze, ECG spike, HUD pulse, strain actions and rumble. The music follows the same `bpm` and aligns its quarter notes to `beat` (§11). Hush slows it; Fermata stops it.\n\n### 3.4 Vitals, clock, misses\n\n- **Vitals:** 0 to 100. Zero is a flatline and fails the operation. Hazards drain it (§4, §5). At 30 or below: danger state (red vignette, line boil, heartbeat rumble, Bea shouts).\n- **Timer:** per operation. Zero fails the operation. It stays visible, and pulses under 30 s.\n- **Misses:** a MISS adds to a counter. Reaching `missLimit` fails the operation (\"Malpractice\").\n\n### 3.5 Grades and score\n\nEach discrete action (a cut, a shard, a wound, a clamp, a kill) earns a grade, shown as comic lettering with an ink splat at the tip:\n\n| Grade | Base points | Composure |\n|---|---|---|\n| FLAWLESS | 300 | +10 |\n| CLEAN | 150 | +5 |\n| ROUGH | 50 | 0 |\n| MISS | -100 | -10 |\n\nCombo: consecutive CLEAN-or-better multiplies points by `1 + 0.05 × combo`, up to ×2. MISS resets it. ROUGH holds it. On success add `vitals × 20 + seconds left × 5`, plus 1500 for no misses.\n\n### 3.6 Ranks\n\nFinal rank compares the score with the operation's `parScore` (data, set from the bot in §17):\n\n| Rank | Score vs par | Title on the stamp |\n|---|---|---|\n| S | at least 90% | Chief of Surgery |\n| A | at least 75% | Attending |\n| B | at least 55% | Resident |\n| C | at least 35% | Intern |\n| D | below 35% | Malpractice Suit Pending |\n\n### 3.7 Composure and the two gifts\n\nComposure is a 0 to 100 meter, gain scaled by `composureGain`. When full, the Special button fires the current doctor's gift, and the meter empties.\n\n- **The Hush (Ren):** a 0.4 s held breath with everything frozen, then for `hushDuration` seconds, strains, drains, timers and the beat run at `hushTimeScale` while your tip and tools run at full speed. An ink-wash expands from the tip: sumi-e monochrome, with red the only color left. Music low-passes down to almost nothing; only the slow heartbeat remains.\n- **Sforzando (Ines):** a violent accented hit. All strains are stunned for `sforzandoStun` seconds and lose `sforzandoDamage` of their current HP, and tools work at `sforzandoToolMult` for `sforzandoDuration` seconds. Comic cut-in, one huge orchestral stab.\n\nIn Operation 7 both surgeons are in the room: D-pad Right (or R) swaps the lead doctor and therefore the gift. In Free Play, the chart offers either doctor.\n\n### 3.8 Difficulty, checkpoints, assist\n\n| | Easy (Intern) | Normal (Resident) | Hard (Attending) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Drains | ×0.6 | ×1 | ×1.35 |\n| Timers | ×1.4 | ×1 | ×0.85 |\n| Tolerances (`scalpelTol`, `stitchGateR`, `clampWindow`, `defibWindow`) | ×1.4 | ×1 | ×0.8 |\n| `missLimit` | 12 | 8 | 5 |\n| Strain HP | ×0.85 | ×1 | ×1.2 |\n\nNew Game asks once, in Bea's voice (\"How much do you hate yourself, child?\"), and every chart can change it.\n\n**Checkpoints:** every phase is a checkpoint. Failing offers *Retry phase*, *Restart operation*, *Quit*. Retrying a phase restores vitals to at least 60, the timer to at least 90 s, and the score to the checkpoint's. Phases must be self-contained: a phase's `spawn` lists everything it needs, so `skipTo(phase)` works for retry, dev mode and attract mode alike. Use `ifMissing: true` for what an earlier phase normally leaves behind, such as clamps.\n\n**Bea's Notes (assist):** after failing the same phase twice, Bea offers her notebook: 30% more time, 30% less drain, rank capped at B. It's optional, stated honestly, and shown on the report.\n\n**Hints:** 12 s without progress, Bea says the next step and the right wheel slot flashes. D-pad Down or H asks at any time, at no cost.\n\n### 3.9 Consults\n\nBefore Operations 5, 6 and 7, Ren and Ines each propose a plan, and the player chooses:\n\n| Plan | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| Ren's: careful | Timer +60 s, drains ×0.8, score ×0.9 |\n| Ines's: aggressive | Timer -40 s, tools ×1.25, score ×1.15 |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Tools\n\n**The wheel never changes.** The left stick flick selects at once (deflection over 0.6, 8 sectors, with hysteresis). Keys 1 to 8 and the mouse wheel do the same, and the on-screen wheel is clickable.\n\n| Key | Dir | Tool | Used for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | N | **Scalpel** | Trace incisions; sever tethers and chain links |\n| 2 | NE | **Forceps** | Pull shards, tumors, stunned parasites; carry to tray |\n| 3 | E | **Suction** | Drain pools |\n| 4 | SE | **Gel** | Disinfect zones, heal scratches, clear spores, seal nests |\n| 5 | S | **Suture** | Stitch wounds through gates |\n| 6 | SW | **Laser** | Burn nodules and parasites, with analog power |\n| 7 | W | **Scanner** | Reveal and mark hidden things; pin Tremolo |\n| 8 | NW | **Clamp** | Stop spurting bleeders on the beat |\n\nOff the wheel: **Syringe** (one button, context-sensitive) and **Defibrillator** (event only).\n\n\"Use\" below means R2 (analog 0 to 1) or LMB/Cross (1.0). Using a tool on nothing is harmless, except the Scalpel and Laser on bare tissue. Wrong tool for a target: no penalty, Bea comments. Every rule below needs a Node test (§17).\n\n- **Scalpel.** Hold Use within 0.08 of a highlighted incision's start. Progress advances by projecting the tip onto the polyline while within `scalpelTol`.\n  - Leaving tolerance while holding: a **nick**: MISS, vitals -3, small pool spawns. Progress is kept; re-enter to continue.\n  - Speed over 1.8 fu/s caps the grade at ROUGH.\n  - Grade by mean deviation: up to 0.35×tol FLAWLESS, up to 0.7×tol CLEAN, else ROUGH.\n  - **Sever:** with Use held, the tip's path crossing a tether or chain link at 0.4 fu/s or more cuts it.\n  - On bare tissue, moving with Use held: a nick every 0.4 s.\n- **Forceps.** Use at 0.4 or more within 0.1 of a grabbable item grips it. Dropping below 0.4 lets go. Embedded items have an `axis` and an `embed` distance (0.15 to 0.6).\n  - Pull within ±50° of the axis at or under `pullTol`: the item comes out, stretching on a Rapier spring, and pops free at full distance.\n  - Pull faster: **tear**: MISS, vitals -4, item stays.\n  - Wrong direction: it just resists.\n  - Held items hang from the tip on a spring. Releasing over the tray deposits them, and they physically land and pile up. Releasing elsewhere: it falls, vitals -1, grab it again.\n  - Grade by pull smoothness (speed variance).\n- **Suction.** Hold Use: pools within radius 0.16 lose `suctionRate × use` volume per second, and droplets arc into the nozzle. Pool done at under 5%. CLEAN, or FLAWLESS if it never overflowed. A pool over its threshold covers guides and drains 0.4 vitals/s.\n- **Gel.** Hold Use: paints a coverage grid (cell 0.04 fu) within `gelRadius`. A zone completes at 90%. Scratches heal when coated for 1 s. Spores clear on contact. Nest holes seal after 1 s. Grade against the zone's par time.\n- **Suture.** A wound shows numbered gates, alternating sides. Hold Use and pass through them in order, within `stitchGateR`. Each gate pulls a stitch tight with a thread you can see (cosmetic rope, §9.7). Tearing more than 0.3 fu away with Use held: MISS. After the last gate, tie off with a quick flick (1.5 fu/s or more) or Alt within 1.5 s, or it auto-ties and can't be FLAWLESS. Grade by average distance from gate centers.\n- **Laser.** Hold Use: a beam at the tip, DPS `laserDps × use` (use clamped to 0.3 to 1) on a target within 0.05. Off-target on tissue, after 0.25 s grace: scorch mark and `laserBurn × use` vitals/s. Six seconds of continuous fire forces a 1.5 s vent. FLAWLESS if no tissue burned on that target.\n- **Scanner.** Hold Use: an x-ray circle of `scannerRadius` around the tip. Hidden things inside are visible; 0.6 s of dwell **marks** them (ping), and they stay marked. Hidden things can't be touched by other tools until marked. A dwell of 1 s on Tremolo pins it.\n- **Clamp.** Use within 0.1 of a bleeder. A ring contracts onto each beat: within `clampWindow` of the beat FLAWLESS, within double CLEAN, otherwise ROUGH (it still works). Unclamped bleeders feed a pool and drain 1 vitals/s. Four clamps per operation. **Alt on a clamp removes it**, and clamps must be off before an operation ends, which the objective line states.\n- **Syringe** (Square / D-pad Left / F). Over an injection site: hold 0.8 s to inject (FLAWLESS if finished on a beat). Anywhere else: 0.5 s plunge into the IV for `stabilizerHeal` vitals, then `stabilizerCooldown`. Unlimited doses.\n- **Defibrillator** (event only). During an arrest, paddles appear. Hold both L2 and R2 (LMB+RMB, or J+K) for 1.2 s to charge while a ring grows; release both as the ring crosses the gold band (`defibWindow`). \"CLEAR!\" White flash, thump, the heart restarts. Too early or too late: weak shock, 2 s lost.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Strains\n\nEach strain is a musical notation symbol come to life. On first appearance: a comic **strain card** with a dictionary definition, and stave lines crawl across the organ in ink.\n\n| Strain | Card text | Look | Behavior | Counter | Music |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Adagio** | *adagio (adv.): slowly, with great expression. Also: it is eating her liver.* | Fat tube body with five yellow-green stave stripes; note-head on a thin stem | Crawls along a site path at 0.12 fu/s. Beat 1 of each bar leaves a scratch at its tail (max 3; 0.5 vitals/s each). Core glows on beats 3 to 4: Laser ×3 there. HP 100 | Laser to 0: stunned 5 s. Forceps it to the tray in time, or it wakes with 40 HP. Grande variant: HP 150, two tails, opens bleeders | Slow cello-like line |\n| **Staccato** | *staccato (adj.): short, sharp, detached. Like her prognosis.* | Tiny black note-heads with flags popping out of holes | Holes spawn notes on off-beats. A note lives 2 beats, then bursts into a spore patch (-2 vitals, then 0.3 vitals/s until gelled). Hidden nests need the Scanner | Laser tap (0.25 s) or Forceps pinch (0.2 s). Gel 1 s (or 3 stitches) seals a nest | Pizzicato on the off-beats |\n| **Tremolo** | *tremolo (n.): a rapid, nervous trembling. Yours, specifically.* | Pale moth with stave-striped wings, always shivering | Flees at 0.9 fu/s if the tip is within 0.6 fu moving faster than 0.45 fu/s. Scratches 1.5 vitals/s while fleeing. Freezes if you approach slowly | Steady in, Scanner 1 s to pin (3 s), Laser 40 HP. Three pins | Fast high strings |\n| **Legato** | *legato (adj.): smoothly connected, without a break. It does not want a break.* | Bead chain joined by curved slur ribbons, on Rapier rope joints, head pinned | Must be cut **tail first**; the active link glows. Wrong link: two links re-heal, vitals -4. Cut pieces reattach after 8 beats unless carried to the tray. Every 8 beats it jerks and opens a bleeder | Scalpel sever, then Forceps to tray | Gliding portamento lead |\n| **Crescendo** | *crescendo (n.): gradually getting louder. She is about to get very loud.* | A hairpin \"<\" wedge wrapped into a ring that widens | Size grows by `0.02 + 0.015 × live feeders` per second, from 0.2 to 1. Drain `1 + 6 × size` vitals/s. Four feeders (HP 40) regrow every 16 beats. At size 1: **Fortissimo**: -20 vitals, 2 bleeders, size resets to 0.6 | Kill all feeders: the ring shrinks and shows a circular cut line. Scalpel the circle, Forceps it out | One new voice every bar |\n| **Fermata** | *fermata (n.): hold. Hold until the conductor releases you. The conductor is not coming.* | The fermata sign as a giant eye: an arc and a dot, opening on the heart | Stops the heart: music cuts to silence, ECG flat, 8 s arrest clock, vitals hold at 15. While the heart is stopped the eye is exposed | Laser the eye (HP 60, then 80) during the pause, then Defib before the clock ends. Between arrests: inject 3 sites. Finally Forceps | One held organ chord |\n| **Coda** | *coda (n.): the tail. The part that ends everything.* | Coda sign (circle and cross) as a core, two long baton arms on Rapier chains, a tailcoat membrane | **Beat 1 Downbeat:** ring telegraphs one beat early; the baton slams, -6 vitals and a scratch, unless you Laser the baton 0.5 s during the wind-up. **Beat 3 Cue:** spawns 2 notes or a small Adagio. **Every 4th bar a rest:** the core opens for 2 beats, Laser ×3. At 66% and 33% of HP 300 it moves to another site and its arms regrow; sever the arms to reach it. At 0: long Forceps pull (embed 0.6) | All of it | All the motifs in counterpoint, with the Dies Irae (§11) on organ |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Operations\n\nAbout 40 to 55 minutes in total. Timers are for Normal. **Every operation also has a chart:** patient name, age, job, one personal gag, and a one-line follow-up shown on the report. Phases are data (§15.3).\n\n### Op 1: Broken Glass (Chapter 1, Ren). Forearm. Timer 4:00, 72 bpm\n*Desmond Pell, 52, taxi driver. \"Has never once used a turn signal.\" Follow-up: \"Discharged Tuesday. Was seen signaling, once, leaving the car park.\"*\nTeaches: tip, wheel, Gel, Forceps, Suction, Syringe, Scanner, Suture.\n1. **Clean:** one gel zone (1.2 × 0.5).\n2. **Pull the glass:** 4 shards (embed 0.18 to 0.3). Each pull leaves a small pool (volume 0.4). Done when shards are on the tray and pools are drained.\n3. **Vitals dip:** scripted -25. Bea teaches the Syringe. Can't fail here.\n4. **The missing shard:** Bea: \"Count them, child.\" Scanner finds a deep shard. Forceps it.\n5. **Stitch:** two lacerations (6 and 8 gates).\n6. **Finish:** gel zone over the stitches.\n\n### Op 2: Growth Spurt (Chapter 1, Ren). Abdomen skin, then bowel. Timer 5:30, 76 bpm\n*Hana Ito, 38, crane operator. \"Lifts forty tons for a living. Fainted at the consent form.\" Follow-up: \"Back on the cranes. Asks after you. Has asked you to stop visiting.\"*\nTeaches: Scalpel, Clamp, Laser, sever, the Hush.\n1. **Open:** gel zone, one long incision (1.4 fu). Ink wipe to the bowel site.\n2. **Bleed:** 2 bleeders and rising pools. Clamp and Suction.\n3. **Nodules:** 5 (HP 30), one hidden. Laser, Scanner.\n4. **The tumor:** sever 3 tethers. Forceps it out (embed 0.4) and carry it to the tray.\n5. **The crash:** the tumor twitches on the tray. Vitals forced to 35, drain 3/s, a rupture wound (12 gates) opens, Composure is set to 100. Bea screams. The Hush prompt appears. The moment it fires: silence, ink wash, the first time. Suture the rupture.\n6. **Close:** take clamps off, close incision (10 gates), gel.\n\n### Op 3: Adagio (Chapter 2, Ren). Liver. Timer 5:30, 80 bpm\n*Theodora Lund, 61, cellist, Harbor Orchestra. \"Refuses anesthesia until she has finished a sentence about Brahms.\" Follow-up: \"Performed in April. Dedicated the slow movement to 'the nervous one.'\"*\n1. **Open:** gel, short incision.\n2. **Look closer:** Scanner sweep reveals stave lines and 3 nests. Adagio emerges, with strain card.\n3. **Adagio:** Laser it down, Forceps it out.\n4. **Eggs:** 4 hidden (Scanner, Laser HP 20) hatching at bar 16 into small Adagios.\n5. **Adagio Grande:** HP 150, two tails, opens bleeders. Clamp them.\n6. **Close:** clamps off, suture 2 lesions, gel.\n\n### Op 4: Staccato (Chapter 2, Ren). Bowel, then stomach. Timer 6:00, 84 bpm\n*Ruben Castellano, 47, ferry captain. \"Has not missed a crossing in nineteen years. Is asking whether this will take long.\" Follow-up: \"Missed two crossings. Sent flowers to the ferry.\"*\n1. **Open:** gel, incision.\n2. **Wave 1:** 8 notes from 3 open holes.\n3. **Find the nests:** Scanner, 4 hidden holes. Seal them.\n4. **Waves 2 and 3:** 12 and 16 notes, faster.\n5. **Tremolo:** first appears here. Bea: \"Slow hands, child.\" Three pins.\n6. **Close:** suture, gel.\n\n### Op 5: Legato (Chapter 3, **Ines**). Brain. Timer 6:00, 70 bpm. Consult\n*Nadia Bloom, 29, school music teacher. \"Her class has sent a card signed 'Please do not miss.'\" Follow-up: \"Back with Class 4. They have sent a second card.\"*\nTeaches: Sforzando, the Consult. Scalpel tolerance ×0.75 throughout: this is a brain.\n1. **Consult,** then **membrane:** two precise arcs.\n2. **Legato:** 12 links, tail first. Forceps each piece away.\n3. **Thrash:** at link 6 it speeds up. Composure set to 100. Ines: \"Now you will see how I work.\" Sforzando prompt.\n4. **Second chain:** 8 links with two tails.\n5. **Repair:** suture the vessel (tight gates), clamps off, gel.\n\n### Op 6: Fermata (Chapter 3, Ren, with Ines on comms). Heart. Timer 7:00, 88 bpm. Consult\n*Dr. Augustin Bell, 66, Chief of Surgery. \"Has told every surgeon in this hospital to quit smoking. Smokes.\" Follow-up: \"Under arrest. Recovering. Has quit.\"*\n1. **Consult,** then **open:** long incision, gel.\n2. **Crescendo:** feeders, Fortissimo threat, cut out the ring.\n3. **First arrest:** the eye opens, silence. Defib tutorial, no Laser needed.\n4. **Antidote:** 3 injection sites, plus bleeders.\n5. **Second arrest:** Laser the eye (HP 60), then Defib.\n6. **Third arrest:** Laser the eye (HP 80), Defib, then Forceps the eye out.\n7. **Close:** clamps off, suture, gel.\n\n### Op 7: Coda (Chapter 4, Ren and Ines). Heart, lung, aorta. Timer 9:30, 92 bpm. Consult\n*Beatrice Okonkwo, 58, Head Nurse. The chart is in her own handwriting: \"Do not be dramatic about this.\" Follow-up: \"Discharged herself. Is back in OR 3. Has opinions.\"*\nBea is unconscious, so **Pip reads her notebook aloud** as the in-operation voice. This is the emotional core: every bark is her words in someone else's voice (\"'Suction before you can't see.' She underlined that twice.\").\n1. **Exposition (heart):** Adagio and Staccato together.\n2. **Development (lung, breathing field):** Tremolo and a Legato chain. Bleeders open back at the heart: site-switch alarm, teaching L1/R1.\n3. **Recapitulation (aorta):** Crescendo, then a Fermata arrest. Every monitor in the hospital audibly falls into time with hers.\n4. **Coda (moves across all three sites):** the Conductor (§5).\n5. **Closing:** wounds on all three sites, clamps off.\n6. **The last stop:** her heart stops, no warning and no hint. Composure refills. Hush, total silence, one Defib. Then her ECG, alone, and the music returns on the downbeat.\n\n### Last Patient (Chapter 4 ending, Ren). Heart. No HUD, no timer, can't fail\n*Dr. Cordelia Voss, 71. Chart: \"Deceased,\" with a date 17 years ago, crossed out by Ren.*\nForty seconds. Rain. One clamp, one Defib. Report: no rank, only: \"Tonight's patient survived.\"\n\n### Grand Rounds (co-op, Free Play and hub rounds). Liver, bowel, heart. Timer 5:00\n*Ngaio Reeve, 55, harbor master. \"Ward Zero has only this to say: everyone scrub in.\"*\nEach movement overlaps the last, so a team can split up. Counts scale by `1 + 0.5 × (players - 1)`, strain HP by `1 + 0.25 × (players - 1)`.\n1. **0:00, Open up:** 2 incisions (liver), 4 shards (bowel), gel zones, 2 bleeders.\n2. **1:00, Adagio and Staccato:** Adagio on the liver, a nest on the bowel.\n3. **2:15, Legato and Crescendo:** a chain on the bowel, a ring on the heart.\n4. **3:30, Fermata:** one arrest. **Any one surgeon's shock counts**; simultaneous shocks earn a bonus. Injection sites at every site.\n5. **4:15, Close:** wounds everywhere. Done before the timer: win.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Story\n\nDelivered as short scenes on a 3D stage (§9.5). Hold Cross/Space to fast-forward; a skip prompt skips the scene. Write 250 to 350 lines in total, in `src/data/story/`. The beats and must-have lines below are fixed; the rest is yours. Chapter names: **Scrub In**, **First Movement**, **Dissent**, **Coda**.\n\n| # | When | Where | Beats and must-have lines |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| S0 | New game | OR 3 at night, empty | An envelope slides under the door. The monitor switches itself on and beeps, in time, with no patient. A silhouette lifts a baton. Title sting. 20 s |\n| S1 | Before Op 1 | Corridor, OR 3 | Flood traffic has stranded the attending. Ren is all there is. \"Thirty-one years in this room, child…\" \"Then simulate faster.\" |\n| S2 | After Op 1 | Bell's office | Bell's praise and warning: \"the best hands… the worst nerves.\" |\n| S3 | Before Op 2 | OR 3 gallery | The board watches. Routine tumor. Ren throws up, apologizes to the bucket |\n| S4 | After Op 2 | OR 3, tray | Ren: \"Everything went quiet.\" Bea goes very still: \"I have heard of that once.\" The tumor twitches on the tray. In rhythm. Sting: \"Tumors do not keep time, child.\" |\n| S5 | Before Op 3 | Pip's pathology lab | Envelope: sheet music addressed to \"The Surgeon on Call\". Pip plays it on a keyboard: \"It's a heartbeat…\" Ambulance: a cellist |\n| S6 | After Op 3 | Ward Zero | Sorrento: \"Please wash your hands.\" REQUIEM, spelled out in full, with a straight face. The Conservatory. Ren is drafted |\n| S7 | Before Op 4 | Ambulance bay, rain | Second score. A ferry captain |\n| S8 | After Op 4 | OR 3 | The OR phone rings. A breathy voice: \"Bravo, Doctor. You play by ear. How delightful.\" Bea drops a full instrument tray. Sting |\n| S9 | Before Op 5 | Ward Zero, then OR 3 | Ines arrives under guard. \"Your hands are excellent. Your diagnosis is a eulogy.\" She operates; you play her. First Consult |\n| S10 | After Op 5 | Hospital roof, rain | Ines explains: she studied under the Maestro, who had the Hush. \"At the end, Doctor, you cannot turn it off.\" \"He waits for the silence. I do not wait.\" |\n| S11 | Before Op 6 | Boardroom | Bell collapses mid-aphorism. The envelope's dedication: \"For Augustin, who signed.\" Consult |\n| S12 | After Op 6 | Recovery room | Bell confesses: he signed Voss's death certificate after the Ward Zero fire, knowing she lived, and funded St. Alder with her research. \"Every heart I mended in this building, I mended with her money.\" Bea: \"I handed her the knife for eleven years. I would know that silence anywhere.\" |\n| S13 | Before Op 7 | OR 3 | Final envelope. Pip plays it and slows down: \"I know this rhythm. It has a murmur. Ms. Okonkwo, you've got a—\" Bea, turning, mid-sentence: \"Forceps, ch—\" She falls. Ines: \"You cannot operate. You love her.\" Ren: \"Then you had better hand me the instruments.\" Pip, shaking, picks up the notebook. Consult |\n| S14 | After Op 7 | Recovery, dawn | Bea wakes. Long silence. \"You held the forceps wrong, child.\" |\n| S15 | Then | OR 3 gallery | Voss, conducting behind the glass, falters: her own heart is the first host. Sorrento: \"Let her go, Doctor.\" Ren: \"She's a patient.\" Leads into Last Patient |\n| S16 | After | St. Alder, morning | Patients' cameos. Pip in student-nurse scrubs. Bea, with a cane, watches Ren close a wound and, for the only time in the game: **\"Good hands, child.\"** Credits roll over attract footage. Post-credits: a new envelope, marked *Opus 2* |\n\n**Bea's barks.** Write 60 or so short in-operation lines keyed by event, each with an expression: `start, nextPhase, toolHint(tool), idle, vitalsLow, vitalsCritical, miss, streak, bleeding, overflow, clampReminder, strainIntro(strain), hushReady, timerLow, success, fail`. A taste:\n\n- `vitalsLow`: \"Pressure's falling, child. Stabilizer.\"\n- `miss`: \"That was not on the line.\" / \"I saw that. God saw that.\"\n- `streak`: \"…Adequate.\" (her highest praise)\n- `bleeding`: \"You are swimming in it. Suction.\"\n- `clampReminder`: \"Your clamps are still in. Would you like to sign for them?\"\n- `timerLow`: \"Clock, child.\"\n- `fail`: \"Call it. Then go and tell the family. No. You. That is the job.\"\n\n**Lint:** a test fails if any Bea line contains \"good\" outside S16. In Op 5 Bea still calls the barks and trades shots with Ines. In Op 6 Ines interjects over comms. In Op 7 every bark comes from Pip reading the notebook.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Controls and screens\n\n### 8.1 Operating controls\n\nDesign order: DualSense, then keyboard and mouse, then the Xbox glyph option. Same game, same screens.\n\n| Action | DualSense | Xbox glyphs | Keyboard and mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Move the tip | Right stick | RS | Mouse. Keyboard-only: WASD or arrows |\n| Pick a tool | Left stick flick | LS | 1 to 8, mouse wheel, click the wheel |\n| Use (analog) | R2 (Cross = full) | RT (A) | LMB (J or Enter on keyboard only) |\n| Steady | L2 | LT | Shift |\n| Alt: drop, remove clamp, tie off | Circle | B | RMB (K) |\n| Syringe | Square or D-pad Left | X or D-pad Left | F |\n| Special | Triangle or D-pad Up | Y or D-pad Up | Space |\n| Site back / next | L1 / R1 | LB / RB | Q / E |\n| Swap lead doctor (Op 7) | D-pad Right | D-pad Right | R |\n| Ask Bea | D-pad Down | D-pad Down | H |\n| Recenter tip | L3 | LS click | C |\n| Pause | Options | Menu | Esc |\n\nThe D-pad mirrors exist so a left thumb can fire Special and Syringe without taking the right thumb off the stick.\n\n**Pads:** accept any pad the browser reports with `mapping === 'standard'`. Read `axes` and `buttons[i].value` directly. If the active pad disconnects mid-operation, pause.\n\n**Rumble:** `vibrationActuator.playEffect('dual-rumble', …)` if present, with a Settings toggle. Heartbeat double-pulse at low vitals, a tick per stitch, a click for clamps, a slam for Defib. Guard against a missing actuator; it can't be tested here beyond the guard.\n\n### 8.2 Menu controls\n\nPad: D-pad or left stick, Cross confirm, Circle back, L1/R1 pages. Keyboard: arrows or WASD, Enter or Space confirm, Esc or Backspace back, Q/E pages. Mouse: hover moves the focus (there's always exactly one focused item), click confirms, wheel scrolls, RMB backs out. In story scenes a click advances the line. In an operation, the wheel's slots and the site tabs are clickable, and the parallax follows the pointer.\n\n### 8.3 Prompts\n\nAll on-screen prompts and any text naming a control use action tokens (`{use}`, `{steady}`, `{special}`), rendered by `src/input/glyphs.js` from the input used last:\n\n- **Pad, PlayStation (default):** drawn SVG shapes for ✕ ○ □ △ in their usual colors; L1/R1/L2/R2 pills; stick icons.\n- **Pad, Xbox** (Settings option): A B X Y in their usual colors; LB/RB/LT/RT.\n- **Keyboard and mouse:** keycaps and mouse-button icons.\n\nDevice switching: any key press means keyboard; mouse movement over 4 px or a click means mouse; a pad button or stick past deadzone means pad. Set `data-prompt-set` on `<html>` so tests can read it. Poll pads every frame; don't rely on `gamepadconnected`.\n\n### 8.4 Ten-foot rules (tests can't judge these; check at 1920×1080)\n\n- Scale all UI with `html { font-size: calc(100vh / 1080 * 16) }` and rem units. It must still hold together in a 640×400 hub frame.\n- Minimum text at 1080p: 34 px anywhere, 44 px dialogue, 40 px objective, 56 px or more for key numbers.\n- 5% safe margin. At most 7 items on a screen. One focus. No hover-only information. No tables outside dev mode.\n- Nothing web-like: no scrollbars, no small links, no dense data screens.\n\n### 8.5 The operating HUD\n\nThe field fills the center, and the HUD holds the edges.\n\n| Where | What |\n|---|---|\n| Top center | Big ECG trace (2D canvas) sweeping on the beat clock, vitals number, bpm |\n| Top left | Objective line and icons of the tool(s) needed |\n| Top right | Timer, score, combo. Co-op: seat chips (name in seat color, current tool) |\n| Bottom left | The tool wheel, large and always visible. Next tool's slot flashes after a hint |\n| Bottom center | Bea's portrait (live 3D bust, §9.6) and her bark in a comic bubble |\n| Bottom right | Composure gauge, Syringe cooldown, button prompts for the current context |\n| Bottom center, above portrait | Site tabs (only with more than one site), with an alarm icon when another site is in trouble |\n\n### 8.6 Screens\n\n| Screen | Contents |\n|---|---|\n| Boot | Instant HTML \"Scrubbing in…\" with a pure-CSS ECG, before the heavy script runs |\n| Touch notice | Touch-only devices. See below |\n| Title | 3D lamp swings on over OR 3 and a heartbeat; logo with an ECG line through it; \"Press any button\" |\n| Main Menu | Story (Continue / New), Free Play, Grand Rounds, Settings, About & Credits. At most 5 big items |\n| Story | §9.5 |\n| Chart | Before each operation: patient bust, name, job, gag, difficulty, Consult when due, big Begin |\n| Operation | §8.5 |\n| Pause | Resume, Retry Phase, Restart Operation, Settings, Dev Mode, Quit to Menu. Opens by itself when the tab is hidden (outside sessions) |\n| Fail | FLATLINE or TIME or MALPRACTICE in huge lettering, Bea's line, Retry Phase / Restart / Quit, Bea's Notes offer when due |\n| Report | A 3D clipboard. Only four stats (Time, Vitals, Precision, Best Combo), the rank stamp slamming down, the patient's follow-up, Continue |\n| Free Play | Chart Board: patient cards with best rank stamps. A card appears once its operation is reached in Story; dev mode unlocks all. Choose difficulty and doctor |\n| Grand Rounds lobby | Four seat cards. \"Press {confirm} to join\" (§14.5) |\n| Settings | Three pages. **Audio:** Music (default 10%), Effects, Voices. **Controls:** Glyphs (PlayStation/Xbox), Rumble, Aim Assist, Swap Sticks, Tip Speed. **Display:** Fullscreen, Screen Shake, Line Boil, Text Speed |\n| About & Credits | §19 |\n| Credits roll | After S16, skippable, over attract footage |\n| Session card, countdown | §14.4 |\n\n**Touch-only devices.** Detect with `matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches === false` and no pad seen. Don't use `navigator.maxTouchPoints`: it reported 0 on the WebKit iPhone and iPad profiles here. Show **\"Controller or keyboard required\"** and a **Settings** button, and no touch controls of any kind. Settings must work by tap on this path. Attract mode still starts after 10 s idle, behind the notice, and a tap returns to it. A key press or pad press leaves the notice for the normal Title.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Art direction\n\n**Native base:** Three.js `MeshToonNodeMaterial` with a stepped ramp, real lights and shadows, then the ink filter in §16.5. All geometry is made in code. Use the engine's stock generators freely (`RoundedBoxGeometry`, `LatheGeometry`, `TubeGeometry`, `ExtrudeGeometry`, and so on) for generic props. Make new art for what makes the game feel like its source: organs, instruments, strains, characters.\n\nThe look is **a comic-book medical drama**: fat ink lines with a hand-drawn wobble, manga tone in the shadows, glossy candy-colored organs with hard white glints. Stylized and bright, T-rated: blood is glossy crimson paint, never realistic.\n\n### 9.1 Palette\n\n| Use | Color |\n|---|---|\n| Drape and UI teal / OR background | `#17707A` / `#0B2529` |\n| UI paper / ink / marigold / alarm | `#F4F1E8` / `#12081A` / `#F2B134` / `#E8443A` |","numLines":480,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1204}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Yax6QE9YmYVvAEtUnWUjpM","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
